{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Ff6pQQNc6ktk51JRMifSQz","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Paper Framing — distill_sparse_swiglu\n2\t\n3\t## Target Venue\n4\t- Primary: EMNLP 2026 Main (ARR May 2026 cycle)\n5\t- Deadline: 2026-05-25 (ARR submission)\n6\t- Track: Efficient Methods for NLP\n7\t\n8\t## Core Claims\n9\t\n10\t1. **KL-trained sparsity predictors eliminate the need for error compensation networks**: At 50% model-level sparsity on SwiGLU LLMs, a KL-trained contextual predictor (no compensation) matches or exceeds BCE+compensation quality (FastForward-style), eliminating ~256MB of inference-time compensation weights and per-layer GEMV overhead. (Headline claim)\n11\t\n12\t2. **Static and contextual corrections compose additively**: SPON's input-independent bias correction (average-case drift) and KL-trained contextual prediction (per-input variation) provide additive quality recovery. Formalized as modular decomposition c = c_s + c_theta(x) where output-space interaction is negligible.\n13\t\n14\t3. **KL advantage scales quadratically with sparsity**: BCE's per-neuron factorized gradient (mean-field analog) misses joint neuron interactions scaling as O(s^2 D^2). Testable prediction: KL-vs-BCE delta follows approximately quadratic curve in sparsity level s.\n15\t\n16\t## Story Spine\n17\t\n18\t- **Problem**: SwiGLU activation sparsity enables 40-60% neuron pruning at inference, but training-free heuristics (WINA/TEAL) leave a 6+ point quality gap, while learned BCE predictors require auxiliary compensation networks adding ~256MB overhead (FastForward).\n19\t\n20\t- **Existing approaches & gaps**: (a) Training-free (WINA, TEAL, R-Sparse): no training cost, but quality gap too large for deployment. (b) Static learned (SPON): zero-overhead bias correction via KL distillation, but input-independent — cannot adapt to per-input neuron importance. (c) Contextual learned (FastForward): BCE predictor + compensation MLP, near-dense quality but inference overhead from compensation network. Gap: no prior work trains the contextual predictor itself with KL, and no work tests SPON + contextual composition.\n21\t\n22\t- **Our framing / headline question**: \"Does output-distribution distillation (KL) make error compensation unnecessary for contextual sparsity prediction?\" The theoretical grounding: BCE gradient dL_BCE/ds_j depends only on neuron j (factorized), while KL gradient dL_KL/ds_j couples all active neurons through d_logits/dh * dh/dm — a mathematical property of the gradient structure, not an analogy. This means KL captures joint interaction effects that BCE misses, especially at high sparsity.\n23\t\n24\t- **Headline result template**: \"On [benchmarks], a KL-trained predictor at 50% model-level sparsity achieves [X] quality (within [Y]% of dense) without compensation, while BCE+compensation achieves [Z] — demonstrating that output-distribution distillation subsumes error compensation and enabling a simpler, lighter inference pipeline.\"\n25\t\n26\t## Two-Dimensional Taxonomy (Conceptual Contribution)\n27\t\n28\t|                          | Decomposed (BCE) | Intermediate (MSE) | Coupled (KL) |\n29\t|--------------------------|:-----------------:|:-------------------:|:-------------:|\n30\t| Static                   | —                 | —                   | SPON          |\n31\t| Per-layer                | —                 | ShadowLLM           | —             |\n32\t| Per-token contextual     | FastForward       | —                   | **Ours**      |\n33\t\n34\tPositions prior work and ours on two axes: correction granularity (static -> per-token) x training signal coupling (decomposed/BCE -> coupled/KL). Highlights the empty cell we fill.\n35\t\n36\t## Anticipated Reviewer Concerns\n37\t\n38\t1. **\"Incremental / no new algorithm\"**: Mitigate with formal decomposition framework, quantitative s^2 prediction, and comprehensive four-quadrant outcome analysis showing all results are informative.\n39\t\n40\t2. **\"KL wins only because more compute\"**: Addressed by iso-compute ablation (plan_003) giving BCE equivalent total FLOPs via extended training.\n41\t\n42\t3. **\"Gumbel-sigmoid relaxation, not KL, explains gains\"**: Addressed by STE baseline (plan_002) isolating loss function contribution.\n43\t\n44\t4. **\"Single model / limited generalization\"**: Addressed by Mistral-7B-v0.3 validation (plan_009) with different architecture (GQA + sliding window).\n45\t\n46\t5. **\"Effect size may not hold on downstream tasks\"**: Full benchmark suite (PPL + 5 discriminative + LongBench + MT-Bench) covers all axes.\n47\t\n48\t## Venue Fit\n49\t- EMNLP Efficient Methods track: strong fit for activation sparsity + distillation\n50\t- NLP-specific evaluation (LongBench long-context, MT-Bench multi-turn) strengthens EMNLP fit vs pure systems venue\n51\t- Novelty ceiling acknowledged: contribution is systematic composition + rigorous ablation, not new architecture\n52\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"19d5cd1b-dd8d-4261-868b-cdcfcf2d032f","uuid":"ad611fc2-8f98-442e-b5b3-b3e5f4a2ee22","timestamp":"2026-05-18T17:07:20.344Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_14/projects/distill_sparse_swiglu/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260515T164925/median/paper_framing.md","content":"# Paper Framing — distill_sparse_swiglu\n\n## Target Venue\n- Primary: EMNLP 2026 Main (ARR May 2026 cycle)\n- Deadline: 2026-05-25 (ARR submission)\n- Track: Efficient Methods for NLP\n\n## Core Claims\n\n1. **KL-trained sparsity predictors eliminate the need for error compensation networks**: At 50% model-level sparsity on SwiGLU LLMs, a KL-trained contextual predictor (no compensation) matches or exceeds BCE+compensation quality (FastForward-style), eliminating ~256MB of inference-time compensation weights and per-layer GEMV overhead. (Headline claim)\n\n2. **Static and contextual corrections compose additively**: SPON's input-independent bias correction (average-case drift) and KL-trained contextual prediction (per-input variation) provide additive quality recovery. Formalized as modular decomposition c = c_s + c_theta(x) where output-space interaction is negligible.\n\n3. **KL advantage scales quadratically with sparsity**: BCE's per-neuron factorized gradient (mean-field analog) misses joint neuron interactions scaling as O(s^2 D^2). Testable prediction: KL-vs-BCE delta follows approximately quadratic curve in sparsity level s.\n\n## Story Spine\n\n- **Problem**: SwiGLU activation sparsity enables 40-60% neuron pruning at inference, but training-free heuristics (WINA/TEAL) leave a 6+ point quality gap, while learned BCE predictors require auxiliary compensation networks adding ~256MB overhead (FastForward).\n\n- **Existing approaches & gaps**: (a) Training-free (WINA, TEAL, R-Sparse): no training cost, but quality gap too large for deployment. (b) Static learned (SPON): zero-overhead bias correction via KL distillation, but input-independent — cannot adapt to per-input neuron importance. (c) Contextual learned (FastForward): BCE predictor + compensation MLP, near-dense quality but inference overhead from compensation network. Gap: no prior work trains the contextual predictor itself with KL, and no work tests SPON + contextual composition.\n\n- **Our framing / headline question**: \"Does output-distribution distillation (KL) make error compensation unnecessary for contextual sparsity prediction?\" The theoretical grounding: BCE gradient dL_BCE/ds_j depends only on neuron j (factorized), while KL gradient dL_KL/ds_j couples all active neurons through d_logits/dh * dh/dm — a mathematical property of the gradient structure, not an analogy. This means KL captures joint interaction effects that BCE misses, especially at high sparsity.\n\n- **Headline result template**: \"On [benchmarks], a KL-trained predictor at 50% model-level sparsity achieves [X] quality (within [Y]% of dense) without compensation, while BCE+compensation achieves [Z] — demonstrating that output-distribution distillation subsumes error compensation and enabling a simpler, lighter inference pipeline.\"\n\n## Two-Dimensional Taxonomy (Conceptual Contribution)\n\n|                          | Decomposed (BCE) | Intermediate (MSE) | Coupled (KL) |\n|--------------------------|:-----------------:|:-------------------:|:-------------:|\n| Static                   | —                 | —                   | SPON          |\n| Per-layer                | —                 | ShadowLLM           | —             |\n| Per-token contextual     | FastForward       | —                   | **Ours**      |\n\nPositions prior work and ours on two axes: correction granularity (static -> per-token) x training signal coupling (decomposed/BCE -> coupled/KL). Highlights the empty cell we fill.\n\n## Anticipated Reviewer Concerns\n\n1. **\"Incremental / no new algorithm\"**: Mitigate with formal decomposition framework, quantitative s^2 prediction, and comprehensive four-quadrant outcome analysis showing all results are informative.\n\n2. **\"KL wins only because more compute\"**: Addressed by iso-compute ablation (plan_003) giving BCE equivalent total FLOPs via extended training.\n\n3. **\"Gumbel-sigmoid relaxation, not KL, explains gains\"**: Addressed by STE baseline (plan_002) isolating loss function contribution.\n\n4. **\"Single model / limited generalization\"**: Addressed by Mistral-7B-v0.3 validation (plan_009) with different architecture (GQA + sliding window).\n\n5. **\"Effect size may not hold on downstream tasks\"**: Full benchmark suite (PPL + 5 discriminative + LongBench + MT-Bench) covers all axes.\n\n## Venue Fit\n- EMNLP Efficient Methods track: strong fit for activation sparsity + distillation\n- NLP-specific evaluation (LongBench long-context, MT-Bench multi-turn) strengthens EMNLP fit vs pure systems venue\n- Novelty ceiling acknowledged: contribution is systematic composition + rigorous ablation, not new architecture\n","numLines":52,"startLine":1,"totalLines":52}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01Me4HuTvcpW7Zv9nKbcj651","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The user wants me to generate detailed median-tier predicted data for each \"planned\" experiment in the experiment_plan. Let me identify which experiments are in \"planned\" status:\n\nLooking at the experiment_plan:\n- plan_010: LongBench English subset evaluation - status: planned\n- plan_011: MT-Bench evaluation - status: planned\n- plan_012: RULER synthetic long-context evaluation - status: planned\n\nAnd the what_if experiments:\n- c1_ablation_bce_kl_targets\n- dense_sparsity_sweep_6pt\n- bce_30pct_forced_topk\n\n\n- kl_sparsity35_s42 - RUNNING\n- c1_ablation_eval_uniform - RUNNING\n- kl_sparsity45_s42 - RUNNING\n- bce_30pct_s42_eval - PREPARING\n- kl_sparsity25_s123 - PREPARING\n- kl_sparsity25_s42_eval_teal - PREPARING\n- kl_sparsity35_s42_eval_teal - PREPARING\n- kl_sparsity45_s42_eval_teal - PREPARING\n\nWait, I need to re-read the user's request more carefully—they're asking specifically for the \"planned\" experiments, not the ones that are already running or preparing.\n\nLooking at the experiment_plan entries, I can see plans 010 through 012 are marked as planned. There's also a what_if section with some additive experiments listed, though some of those appear to already be completed or have zero estimated GPU hours, which suggests they might already be done or are duplicates of existing runs. are the currently running/preparing 25/35/45 experiments)\n- bce_30pct_forced_topk (estimated_gpu_hours: 0.0 — bce_30pct_s42 and bce_30pct_s42_eval)\n\nThe what_if experiments show zero estimated GPU hours, which suggests they might not require additional resources since the underlying data already exists or they're leveraging existing runs. Still, they'll need predictions generated.\n\nLooking at the request more carefully, I need to generate median-tier predictions for all experiments in the plan (plan_001 through plan_012) plus the what_if variants. For the DONE experiments, the predictions should align with the median tier definition rather than necessarily matching their actual results. Scanning through the statuses, plan_010, plan_011, and plan_012 are marked as planned and definitely need predictions, while the earlier ones are already completed. I need to clarify what the user is actually asking for. They want detailed predictions for the experiments marked as \"planned\" in the experiment_plan — that's plan_010, plan_011, and plan_012. But I should also account for the experiments currently running or preparing, since those will eventually need results too. The what_if section contains three hypothetical scenarios that also need predictions with explanations for why those results would occur at those specific parameter settings. _s42_eval_teal - PREPARING\n\nThese are part of the what_if experiments (dense_sparsity_sweep_6pt, c1_ablation_bce_kl_targets, bce_30pct_forced_topk).\n\nLet me map the experiments:\n\nFor the what_if \"c1_ablation_bce_kl_targets\":\n- Already done: c1_ablation_bce_kl_targets_s42 (PPL 9.29 at per-token ~60.9% sparsity)\n- Running: c1_ablation_eval_uniform (uniform protocol evaluation)\n\nFor \"dense_sparsity_sweep_6pt\":\n- Already done: 25% (kl_sparsity25_s42), 30% (kl_sparsity30_s42_v3), 40% (d030_kl40_uniform), 50% (multiple)\n- Running: 35% and 45% sparsity checkpoints\n- Eval pending: the 25%, 35%, and 45% variants need teal evaluation\n\nFor \"bce_30pct_forced_topk\":\n- Training complete for the 30% BCE variant\n- Evaluation still pending\n\nSo I need to provide predictions for LongBench, MT-Bench, and RULER evaluations, plus the c1_ablation_bce_kl_targets what_if specifically.\n\nNow I'm setting up the median-tier predictions with these key parameters: small PPL gaps around 0.15-0.5 for the C1 ablation under uniform protocol, BCE 30% forced TEAL PPL in the 10.0-12.0 range, regime piecewise R² between 0.85-0.95, KL 30% uniform MMLU 3-seed mean around 0.37-0.40 with standard deviation 0.015-0.025, and expected reviewer average of 3.5-4.0 for acceptance.\n\nThe narrative here is that C1 acts as the primary driver accounting for roughly 85-95% of the effect, but gradient coupling still contributes something measurable. Regime boundaries are present though somewhat noisy. For the C1 ablation eval under uniform protocol at 50% sparsity, I'm comparing against the original finding where BCE+KL-targets achieved 9.29 PPL at about 60.9% per-token sparsity, and now I need to estimate what happens when we shift to uniform protocol at 50% sparsity—this should show a modest PPL increase compared to the optimized Gumbel approach.\n\nLooking at the uniform 50% baseline of 11.96 PPL from the KL variant, I'd expect BCE+KL-targets to fall somewhere in the 12.1-12.5 range, suggesting gradient coupling adds a small but detectable effect. For the dense sparsity sweep across six points, I'm pulling together the actual TEAL global PPL values across the sparsity levels to see the full trajectory. Looking at the interpolated PPL values across the remaining sparsity levels—45% should land around 10.8-11.2 between the 40% and 50% benchmarks. For the regime analysis, the median tier suggests boundaries exist but with noise, showing piecewise R² between 0.85-0.95 with roughly ±5% sparsity tolerance. Now examining the BCE forced top-k scenario at 30%: since the native BCE model at 50% has a TEAL PPL of 27.97, forcing it down to 30% should improve performance, with median tier predictions putting it in the 10.0-12.0 range—which would narrow KL's advantage considerably.\n\nMoving to the LongBench evaluation, I'm comparing dense baselines against KL at 30% uniform sparsity across narrativeqa, qasper, gov_report, and multi_news tasks. The median tier requires predictions across multiple conditions including KL-only, BCE with compression, SPON variants, and training-free baselines. Long-context tasks are inherently harder for sparse models, so I expect KL to show degradation relative to dense but still outperform the BCE and TEAL baselines. For MT-Bench, I'm looking at 80 multi-turn questions across 8 categories, with the dense LLama-3.1-8B baseline likely scoring around 6.5-7.0.\n\nFor RULER's synthetic long-context tasks at 4K and 16K tokens, sparsity will show clear capability degradation. Now I'm thinking through the downstream MMLU scores for the 6-point sparsity sweep. At 25% sparsity, MMLU should improve over the 30% result of 0.4061, so I'm estimating 0.43-0.47. At 35%, it should fall between 30% and 40% uniform (0.3068), landing around 0.35-0.37. At 45%, I'm looking at the range between 40% and 50%, probably 0.29-0.31. I need to clarify what allocation strategy to use across the sweep.\n\nLooking at the global allocation benchmarks, I can see the performance metrics across different sparsity levels—30% gives MMLU of 0.2527, 40% reaches 0.3806, and 50% drops to 0.2789. The uniform allocation shows different patterns, with 30% at 0.4061. I should use the global allocation data as the baseline for the sweep since that's the standard evaluation protocol.\n\nThe 6-point sweep covers KL predictors at 25% through 50% with regime boundaries precisely mapped, and I need to generate PPL predictions that demonstrate the non-monotonic allocation preference pattern I'm seeing in the data. I see that plan_001 is actually marked as complete despite those remaining runs listed, so those experiments must have finished already. Now I need to focus on generating the YAML output for the planned experiments—plan_010, plan_011, and plan_012—along with the what_if experiments like the ablation study, the 6-point sparsity sweep, and the forced top-k variant. I'm also thinking through what tables and figures these experiments will produce.\n\nLet me construct the full YAML systematically, making sure each experiment has a clear name, plan ID, description, expected results with specific metrics, a solid rationale for those predictions, and details about what tables and figures will come from the analysis.\n\nStarting with the C1 ablation on BCE and KL targets under the uniform evaluation protocol: the key insight is that BCE trained on KL-derived masks should show only a small gap compared to the KL predictor itself, not an exact match. Based on what I've already observed at around 60.9% per-token sparsity where BCE+KL-targets achieved a PPL of 9.29 versus KL's 9.21, I'd expect a similar tight comparison when we enforce exactly 50% sparsity under the uniform protocol.\n\nFor the dense sparsity sweep, I need to fill in the missing points at 25%, 35%, and 45% sparsity levels. Looking at the trend from the data I have, the PPL should follow a smooth curve as sparsity decreases, so I can interpolate and extrapolate reasonably from the 30% and 40% anchor points I already have.\n\nWith six data points across the sparsity range, a piecewise linear fit would give me an R² around 0.85-0.95, which is noisier than the best-case scenario but still reasonable for the median tier. This noisiness in the fit reflects what the median tier specifies—the boundary between budget-surplus and budget-constrained regimes isn't a sharp transition at a single sparsity value.\n\nFor the downstream MMLU results, the interesting part is where uniform allocation stops dominating and global allocation takes over. At low sparsity levels like 25-30%, uniform should perform better, but somewhere around 35-40% the crossover happens, and this transition should be noisy rather than crisp. Now I'm working through the MMLU predictions under global allocation, starting with the 25% sparsity point.\n\nLooking at the actual MMLU values across sparsity levels, there's a strange non-monotonicity: global allocation at 30% sparsity gives 0.2527, which is much worse than at 40% where it reaches 0.3806. This happens because the global allocation strategy distributes pruning differently across layers at each sparsity level—at 30% it starves the late layers more aggressively, while at 40% the learned allocation becomes more balanced and actually performs better.\n\nIn contrast, uniform allocation shows a clean monotonic decline as sparsity increases, suggesting the allocation strategy itself is the key variable here rather than just the total budget.\n\nFor the regime analysis, the median tier results indicate a piecewise relationship with R² around 0.85-0.95, though the boundary between regimes is noisy with roughly ±5% tolerance. When I look at the BCE predictor forced to 30% via TEAL top-k, it should perform better than at 50% since the pruning is less aggressive, but the magnitude-based oracle targets it uses are still suboptimal compared to KL-based approaches—the median tier shows a PPL gap of about 1.0-3.0 between BCE and KL at this sparsity level.\n\nThis is notably smaller than the ~10.7 PPL gap at 50%, which supports the pattern that KL's advantage actually increases with higher sparsity levels. For downstream MMLU performance at 30%, I'd expect BCE to land somewhere between 0.35-0.40, falling below KL's uniform allocation but above the global variant depending on how the allocation strategy plays out.\n\nLooking at the LongBench results, I'm noticing something odd in the multi_news scores—they jumped dramatically from 2.5 in the dense model to 18.01 with KL 30% uniform, which seems like a significant degradation rather than the expected pattern.  The other conditions show more consistent trends: BCE+comp underperforms KL across all subtasks at 30%, TEAL baseline is worse than both, and SPON variants add minimal value beyond standard KL pruning.\n\nFor MT-Bench, I'm estimating the dense Llama-3.1-8B baseline around 6.0-6.5 based on typical community benchmarks.\n\nAt 30% sparsity, KL should drop to 5.0-5.5 while TEAL and BCE+comp fall further to 4.0-5.0 range. At 50% sparsity, all methods degrade sharply with KL around 3.0-3.5 and TEAL dropping to 2.0-2.5.\n\nFor RULER's synthetic long-context tasks, dense performance at 4K should be nearly perfect on retrieval, with some degradation at 16K. Under sparsity, KL at 30% shows mild degradation at 4K but moderate degradation at 16K, while 50% sparsity causes significant performance drops regardless of sequence length.\n\nNow I'm ready to compile the full YAML output, though I want to double-check the median tier definitions first.\n\nLooking at the specific metric ranges and performance characteristics across different conditions—C1 ablation shows small gaps under uniform protocol, gradient coupling contributes minor but measurable effects, and regime boundaries exist though with some noise. I'm tracking the key performance indicators like PPL gaps, TEAL performance, regime fit quality, and MMLU scores across seeds to calibrate the expected reviewer assessment and acceptance probability.\n\nNow I'm refining the narrative framing—shifting from \"sole driver\" to \"primary driver\" with quantified contribution ranges, upgrading the gradient coupling analysis from suggestive to a proper decomposition, and recharacterizing the phase transition claim as empirical regime structure. I need to plan 14B model downstream experiments for the rebuttal and decide whether to target the Main track or prepare Findings as a fallback. Let me map out all the planned experiments: LongBench, MT-Bench, and RULER from the main pipeline, plus the what-if scenarios around C1 ablation with different BCE and KL targets, a six-point sparsity sweep, and forced top-k variants for the 30% BCE condition.\n\nI'm tracking which what-if experiments are actively running or preparing—the C1 ablation with uniform evaluation is currently executing, while the KL sparsity variants at 25%, 35%, and 45% are queued up, along with the BCE 30% condition and an additional seed run. Now I need to construct detailed predictions for the median tier: what the perplexity numbers should look like, why they align with my assumptions, which tables and figures they populate, and what the key analytical takeaways are. I'm being careful to keep the LongBench predictions consistent with the existing dense and KL 30% uniform baseline data I've already observed.\n\nLooking at the LongBench scores more closely, the KL 30% uniform results show substantial improvements on narrativeqa and qasper, but the multi_news jump from 2.5 to 18.01 seems like it could be a metric shift rather than a real quality gain—that's worth flagging. For the median tier predictions, I expect KL methods to outperform BCE methods, which in turn beat the TEAL baselines, mirroring the perplexity ordering, though long-context tasks tend to magnify those quality differences. SPON alone contributes minimal gains, so the real value comes from combining it with KL or other stronger methods.\n\nNow I'm mapping out the specific performance expectations for plan_010 across sparsity levels. At 30% sparsity, the dense baseline gives me reference points, and I've already got the actual KL 30% uniform measurements. TEAL should underperform KL, while BCE+comp sits somewhere between them. SPON provides only marginal improvements over the sparse baseline, and SPON+KL essentially matches KL's performance. At 50% sparsity, all methods hit a wall due to capacity constraints, though KL maintains its relative advantage even as the absolute gap shrinks. I have enough information now to construct the YAML configuration.\n\nWriting YAML configuration...\n\nFor the c1_ablation_eval_uniform test, I'm examining how the BCE+KL-targets model performs under the TEAL uniform protocol at 50% sparsity—this is the critical comparison to isolate whether gradient coupling actually matters. Looking at the median tier results, the KL uniform 50% baseline PPL is around 11.92, and I'm estimating the BCE+KL-targets gap at 0.15-0.5 PPL points, which puts the expected performance at roughly 12.25. This gap magnitude tells me whether gradient coupling is negligible (gap near zero), moderately important (0.15-0.5 range suggesting 5-15% contribution), or critical (gap exceeding 0.5).\n\nNow I'm moving to construct the full YAML output and need to predict downstream metrics for the sweep points and the BCE 30% condition. For the 6-point sparsity sweep, I'm training KL predictors at the missing sparsity levels (25%, 35%, 45%) while 30%, 40%, and 50% already have data, then evaluating TEAL global PPL plus downstream tasks across all points. I'm checking what metrics already exist for kl_sparsity25_s42—I have the training loss and actual sparsity, but evaluation metrics haven't been collected yet.\n\nFor the missing evaluation points, I need to predict PPL and downstream performance at 25%, 35%, and 45% TEAL global sparsity using the existing data points at 30%, 40%, 50%, and 70%. Looking at the PPL trend, I'm interpolating between these known values, though I'm noticing some non-monotonicity in the pattern that complicates the linear extrapolation.\n\nAt 25% sparsity, the natural sparsity of SwiGLU neurons is already close to that threshold, so the predictor needs minimal additional pruning work. For downstream tasks, I'm using the existing performance at 30% and 40% global sparsity to estimate the intermediate points, tracking how ARC, WG, MMLU, and GSM8K degrade as sparsity increases.\n\nThe key insight is that MMLU performs much worse at 30% global (0.253) compared to 40% global (0.381) because the global allocation starves the later knowledge layers at tighter budgets. At 25%, with more capacity available, I'd expect MMLU to recover to around 0.45-0.50, with ARC around 0.45-0.48, WG around 0.72-0.74, and GSM8K around 0.10-0.15. At 35%, performance should fall between the 30% and 40% points, with MMLU around 0.30.\n\nNow looking at the intermediate points: at 45% global, performance would sit between 40% and 50%, with MMLU around 0.32-0.35, ARC around 0.36-0.39, WG around 0.64-0.66, and GSM8K around 0.03-0.04. The non-monotonicity in global allocation makes this regime tricky to predict precisely. When I fit this data with median tier noise, the R² of a piecewise fit would be around 0.85-0.95 since the transitions aren't crisp, and the 35% point might not clearly belong to either regime.\n\nI have enough data now to write out the full prediction. Looking at the PPL trajectory across TEAL global allocations, the progression from 25% to 50% appears fairly linear, so the regime structure is more visible in the downstream metrics and allocation preferences. Examining how MMLU performs under global versus uniform allocation reveals the budget-surplus regime clearly—at 30%, uniform substantially outperforms global, but by 35% this gap narrows as the budget constraints tighten.\n\nThe crossover happens between 35% and 40%, where global allocation suddenly outperforms uniform, indicating the shift into the budget-constrained regime. This boundary aligns with what I'd expect from the median tier's noisy regime characterization. Now I'm ready to write the final YAML with the ablation results, the 6-point sparsity sweep with regime analysis, the forced topk configuration, and the plan predictions.\n\nBut I need to reconsider the BCE 30% prediction more carefully. The eval is still preparing, and while BCE at 50% showed massive TEAL perplexity of 27.97, at 30% the pruning is less aggressive. The core issue is that BCE relies on magnitude-based oracle targets, though at lower sparsity levels the overlap between magnitude and KL-based masks increases significantly.\n\nFor the TEAL perplexity at 30%, I'm settling around 10.8—worse than KL 30% at 8.98 but substantially better than BCE 50%. On downstream tasks like MMLU, BCE 30% should land around 0.35-0.40, performing comparably to TEAL 30% since magnitude-based pruning becomes more effective at lower sparsity levels. I need to finalize the YAML output now. For the uniform evaluation at 50% sparsity, BCE trained on KL-derived masks should perform similarly to KL itself since they're using the same masks, though the slight PPL gap suggests marginally worse downstream results—around 0.270-0.275 for MMLU compared to KL's 0.2777. I have all the reasoning I need to write this out.\n\nNow I'm mapping out which experiments contribute to each table and figure in the paper—the main PPL and downstream results, the causal ablation, cross-model generalization across Mistral and Qwen, long-context and multi-turn benchmarks, plus the efficiency frontier, sparsity regime analysis, mask divergence patterns, gradient signal-to-noise ratios, and RULER results.\n\nFor the LongBench experiments, I need to predict results across all the method and sparsity combinations—dense baseline, various KL configurations, BCE with compression, SPON variants, and TEAL at different sparsity levels. The pattern I expect is that KL should preserve long-context quality better than the other sparse methods, though all of them will degrade somewhat on longer sequences, with summarization tasks suffering more than QA since they require broader understanding. For MT-Bench with multi-turn instruction following, I'm estimating the dense Llama-3.1-8B baseline around 6.0-6.5, then working through the KL 30% results.\n\nFor MT-Bench, KL 30% drops to around 5.2-5.5, KL 50% falls further to 3.5-4.0, TEAL 30% lands around 4.5-5.0, TEAL 50% degrades significantly to 2.5-3.0, and BCE with compression at 30% stays relatively stable at 4.8-5.2, while SPON shows minimal gains. On the RULER benchmark at 4K and 16K context lengths, I'm looking at three task types—needle in a haystack, variable tracking, and common words extraction—where dense models perform near-perfectly at 4K (>95%), KL 30% drops to 85-92%, KL 50% falls to 65-75%, and TEAL underperforms KL by about 10-15%. At 16K, the dense baseline ranges from 85-95% depending on the specific task.\n\nNow I'm organizing the complete YAML output by mapping the imagined experiments to their corresponding plan IDs, structuring the list to include the ablation evaluation, various KL sparsity configurations, the combined regime analysis, BCE compression tests, and the three main benchmark plans. Looking at the MMLU performance across sparsity levels, there's a non-monotonic pattern where 30% global allocation (0.253) underperforms 40% (0.381), likely because aggressive pruning at 30% starves the later layers that are critical for MMLU performance. At 25%, the budget should be less constrained, so I'd expect MMLU around 0.40-0.50, while 35% would fall somewhere between 30% and 40% given the intermediate starvation effect. The allocation skew is extreme (late layers get very few neurons). At 40%, global keeps 60% of neurons. The allocation is less skewed, or the predictor has learned to allocate more to critical layers.\n\nLooking at global_30pct_retention: early layers retain ~77% of neurons while late layers only retain ~50%. At 40% global, the retention would be more balanced. At 25% global, even more neurons are kept total (75%), so late layers might retain ~55-60% → MMLU should be better. At 35% global, similar allocation pattern to 30% but slightly less aggressive → late layers retain ~52-55% → MMLU similar or slightly better than 30%.\n\nThe MMLU at 30% global of 0.253 is essentially random (0.25), meaning knowledge is almost completely lost. At 35% global, it might still be near-random or slightly above, with the real recovery happening at 40% when the predictor's allocation pattern shifts qualitatively. The 35% point represents a noisy boundary that could go either way. I'm predicting 25% global MMLU around 0.46 with enough budget for all layers, 35% global around 0.29-0.32 still in the starvation zone but less severe, putting the noisy regime boundary between 35-40%. For uniform allocation, I expect a monotonic decline from 25% (~0.47) through 30% (0.406) to 35% (~0.36) and 40% (0.307).\n\nThe crossover where uniform outperforms global shifts to global outperforming uniform happens between 35-40%, marking the regime boundary. Now I'm calculating the mean and standard deviation for the three-seed uniform MMLU data at 30% to verify consistency with the median tier expectations.\n\nWorking through the three seeds, I get a mean of 0.385 and standard deviation of 0.018, which falls squarely within the expected range for median tier. The actual data checks out. Now I'm drafting the YAML configuration to capture all these findings.\n\nWriting YAML configuration... 调整为 \"primary driver (~85-95% of the gap)\"。C1 uniform eval 显示 BCE+KL-targets PPL 比 KL 高 0.15-0.5，说明 gradient coupling 有 minor 但非零贡献。§3.6 gradient coupling 分析从 suggestive intuition 升级为 quantitative decomposition（~5-15% 贡献）。Claim 3 从 \"phase transition\" 软化为 \"empirical regime structure\"，6-point sweep 的 piecewise fit R²=0.89 显示趋势存在但边界模糊（±5% sparsity 容差）。14B downstream gap（MMLU 64.49 vs TEAL 71.83）需要在 rebuttal 中补充额外的 seed 和 allocation 实验。BCE 30% forced PPL 10.8 缩小了 KL 在低 sparsity 下的优势，但 KL 仍然明确胜出。目标还是争取 Main（reviewer avg ~3.7），但也在准备 Findings fallback（accept probability ~0.35）。现在规划一个 ablation 实验，用 BCE 训练 KL 衍生的 oracle masks，在 TEAL uniform protocol 下以 50% sparsity 评估，作为对照组来隔离不同因素的影响。 Looking at the results, the gradient coupling effect is modest but real. The BCE+KL-targets approach uses the same oracle masks as the pure KL method, but BCE's factorized gradients can't capture the coupled interactions between neurons the way KL's gradient flow through logits and hidden states can. This limitation means the predictor makes slightly different decisions at the boundaries—neurons with activations near the threshold—leading to that ~0.29 PPL gap we're seeing at the median level.\n\nThe gap confirms gradient coupling contributes meaningfully, around 8.5% of the total KL advantage over magnitude baselines, but it's dwarfed by the target quality effect, which accounts for roughly 91.5% of the improvement. The downstream metrics show even smaller gaps because at 50% sparsity the system is already hitting the collapse floor.\n\nLooking at the uniform protocol results, the C1 ablation falls just short of exact match with only a 0.29 PPL gap. This shifts how I should frame the narrative—rather than claiming target quality is the sole driver, I need to position it as the primary driver (85-95%) while acknowledging gradient coupling's measurable but minor contribution. This actually strengthens the paper's credibility since reviewers prefer nuanced claims over absolute ones, and it gives the gradient coupling analysis from section 3.6 proper quantitative grounding instead of just intuitive appeal.\n\nI should add discussion in section 4.4 explaining why gradient coupling's impact is limited—likely because at 50% sparsity, most neuron importance decisions are already clear-cut, and coupled gradients only help disambiguate the borderline ~10-15% of neurons. For the sparsity sweep, I'm filling in the missing data points at 25%, 35%, and 45% sparsity using KL predictors to complete the 6-point evaluation across the full range.\n\nCompiling expected results for perplexity and downstream task performance... Looking at the regime analysis results and the rationale for PPL predictions under different sparsity levels, I'm seeing that the piecewise regime model fits well across metrics, and the PPL growth follows a predictable pattern where lower sparsity regions show slower marginal increases due to the importance distribution of neurons.\n\nFor downstream tasks like MMLU, the global allocation strategy exhibits highly non-monotonic behavior—at 25% sparsity it maintains reasonable performance near 71% of dense baseline, but at 30% it drops sharply because the global top-k selection begins starving the later knowledge-intensive layers of sufficient capacity.\n\nPerformance gradually recovers at 35-40% as the predictor learns better layer-specific priorities to protect critical layers, then declines again at 45-50% when total capacity becomes too constrained. This non-monotonic pattern with multiple regime transitions results in a much lower piecewise R² of 0.72 for MMLU-global compared to PPL, since the allocation artifacts create discontinuities that are harder to fit smoothly.\n\nIn contrast, uniform allocation eliminates layer starvation entirely, producing a cleaner monotonic decline and a higher piecewise R² of 0.96 for MMLU-uniform. Looking at the median sparsity level, the regime boundary sits around 37.5% with some noise, and there's a gradual crossover zone of about 5% width where global allocation transitions from underperforming to outperforming uniform allocation, rather than a sharp switching point.\n\nThe 6-point sparsity sweep across these figures and tables reveals that the median regime exhibits an empirical structure rather than a true phase transition—PPL grows roughly linearly without the quadratic scaling some might expect. Meanwhile, MMLU under global allocation shows a clear non-monotonic pattern, with a sharp performance drop between 25% and 30% due to allocation starvation, followed by recovery in the 30% to 40% range.\n\nI'm identifying three distinct regimes with fuzzy boundaries: a budget-surplus zone up to 30% where uniform allocation stabilizes performance, a budget-constrained middle ground around 35-45% where learned allocation wins out, and a capacity-cliff region above 50% where allocation strategy becomes irrelevant. The piecewise fit achieves an R² of 0.89 across metrics, which clears the 0.85 threshold but falls short of the 0.95 gold standard. I should frame this as \"empirical regime structure\" in the paper rather than \"phase transition,\" and note the ±5% boundary tolerance around each regime transition.\n\nNow I'm setting up the evaluation for the BCE predictor trained on magnitude oracle targets at 30% sparsity using TEAL forced top-k, which fills in the missing data point for Table 2 at the recommended operating point. I'm tracking the expected perplexity, accuracy, and other benchmark metrics across TEAL, ARC-C, WinoGrande, MMLU, and GSM8K, along with reference values from KL and vanilla TEAL baselines to establish the performance gap.\n\nThe median BCE predictor achieves a forced PPL of 10.82 at 30% sparsity, which falls within the expected range because the magnitude-based oracle targets show higher overlap with KL-derived masks at lower sparsity levels (Jaccard similarity around 0.55-0.60 at 30% versus 0.40 at 50%), reducing BCE's typical disadvantage, though the predictor's inherent training characteristics still play a role in the final performance. signal 不 couple neurons 间的交互——在 30% 下虽然大部分 neurons 是 \"easy decisions\"，但仍有 ~10-15% 的 borderline neurons 上 BCE 做出次优决策。TEAL 30% vanilla PPL = 10.97，BCE 30% forced PPL = 10.82 与之接近（仅 -0.15），说明在低 sparsity 下 BCE learned predictor 相比 training-free magnitude pruning 的额外增益极小——这与之前的发现一致，magnitude-based oracle targets 本身就与 training-free magnitude ranking 高度相关，BCE 只是学会了更好地 approximate 这些 targets，而非发现不同的 neuron importance pattern。\n\nKL 30% TEAL global PPL = 8.98，gap = 1.84 PPL，比 50% 下的 gap 小很多但仍然显著，说明即使在低 sparsity 下 KL-derived masks 仍然质量更优——gap 缩小是因为低 sparsity 下 \"easy neurons\" 占比更高，两种 oracle 的分歧集中在少量 borderline neurons 上。在 downstream 指标上，BCE 30% MMLU 0.371 接近 TEAL 30% baseline 0.386，两者都采用 magnitude-based pruning。\n\n这远低于 KL 30% uniform 0.406，再次确认 BCE 在低 sparsity 下不提供超越 training-free baseline 的实质性 downstream 增益。\n\n现在看 BCE 30% PPL 10.82 这个关键数据点——它证明了几个重要的事实：BCE 在推荐 operating point 也表现不佳，相比之下 KL 的 gap 只有 1.84 PPL（相对提升 20.5%）；而且 BCE 几乎等同于 TEAL baseline（gap 仅 0.15），说明它的 learned predictor 在低 sparsity 下基本上就是在复现 magnitude pruning，没有真正超越 training-free 方法的价值。这完全符合之前的分析：KL 发现了根本不同的 mask patterns，而 BCE 只能近似 magnitude-based masks，即使在低 sparsity 下也是如此。\n\n不过审稿人可能会觉得 KL 在低 sparsity 下的优势幅度不够明显，所以需要在论文里强调相对 gap 的 20.5% 和下游任务的实际差异。\n\n接下来要设计 LongBench 的完整评估方案，涵盖 4K-32K 的英文子集，测试 KL-only、BCE+comp、SPON 等多个配置在不同 sparsity 下的表现，并按任务类型（单文档 QA、多文档 QA 等）做细粒度分析。\n\n现在看的是各个任务的预期结果数据，包括 dense baseline 和不同压缩方法在 NarrativeQA、QAsper、GovReport、MultiNews 等任务上的性能指标。 Now I'm looking at the performance metrics for different sparse attention methods across the LongBench datasets at the median setting, and I'm noticing that all the sparse approaches show much larger degradation on long-context tasks than what their short-context perplexity increases would suggest—for instance, KL with 30% uniform sampling only shows a 44% increase in WikiText-2 perplexity.\n\nbut drops 38-40% on tasks like NarrativeQA and GovReport because maintaining attention coherence across the full context window becomes increasingly difficult as sparsity accumulates through layers. I'm also observing that KL 30% consistently outperforms TEAL 30% across all subtasks, with particularly large gaps on QA tasks where precise neuron selection is critical for keeping the query properly aligned with relevant context.\n\nAt 50% sparsity, both methods collapse dramatically—KL drops to just 29% of dense performance on NarrativeQA while TEAL performs even worse at 18%—which aligns with what I'd expect in a capacity-cliff regime where the model simply lacks enough capacity to function. Finally, SPON+KL performs essentially the same as KL alone across all subtasks, confirming that the allocation strategy doesn't provide additional benefits in long-context scenarios.\n\nLooking at the compensation network results, BCE+comp at 30% sparsity slightly outperforms TEAL but falls far short of KL, suggesting that layer-wise compensation has limited value for long-context tasks since it can't address the cross-layer information flow degradation. The MultiNews results are uniformly very low across all methods, which likely reflects an evaluation metric or generation length mismatch rather than a meaningful finding about the pruning approaches.\n\nNow examining the LongBench radar chart across different sparsity levels and task types—KL consistently outperforms the baselines, but all sparse methods show significant quality loss on long-context tasks, with even KL at 30% recovering only around 60% of the dense model's performance. The key defense here is that the predictor was trained on just 2048-token sequences yet generalizes to 4K-32K without additional fine-tuning.\n\nMoving to MT-Bench evaluation with 80 multi-turn questions across 8 categories judged by GPT-4o, testing Dense, KL at both 30% and 50%, TEAL at 30% and 50%, BCE+comp at 30%, and SPON at 50%. teal50_mtbench_avg: 2.78\nbce_comp30_mtbench_avg: 4.85\nspon50_mtbench_avg: 2.93\n# Per-category highlights\nkl30_writing: 5.8\nkl30_reasoning: 4.2\nkl30_math: 3.5\nkl30_coding: 4.8\nkl30_extraction: 6.1\nkl30_stem: 4.5\nkl30_humanities: 5.6\nkl30_roleplay: 6.7\n\nMT-Bench 衡量指令跟随和多轮对话能力。Dense Llama-3.1-8B 的基准约为 6.28，与社区报告的 6.2-6.5 一致。KL 30% 压缩达到约 5.15（密集模型的 82%），保留了指令跟随的核心能力——提取、角色扮演和人文科学等任务表现良好（>5.5），因为这些主要依赖模式匹配和流畅性，对神经元级精度不敏感。但推理（4.2）和数学（3.5）明显下降，因为这些需要精确的中间计算，稀疏性导致的神经元修剪直接破坏了多步推理链的连贯性。KL 50% 压缩跌至约 3.42（仅为密集模型的 54%），符合容量悬崖的特征。\n\n多轮对话中第二轮的质量尤其差，因为模型需要同时关注第一轮上下文并生成连贯的后续回应。\n\nKL 30% 相比 TEAL 30% 的表现（5.15 vs 4.62，+0.53，+11.5%）——差距在 MT-Bench 空间中更小，因为评分采用 1-10 的离散刻度，GPT-4o 评判器对细微质量差异不敏感。BCE+补偿网络 30% 达到 4.85，介于 TEAL 和 KL 之间——补偿网络相比 TEAL 有所改进，但不如 KL 的整体掩码质量。\n\n在中位数档下，MT-Bench 结果\"尚可但不令人印象深刻\"：KL 有一致的优势但绝对性能下降明显。这是支撑核心论述的二级实验，主要作用是证明 KL 的优势延伸到指令跟随能力，部分回应了审稿人的关切，但绝对分数的下降（从 6.28 降至更低）不会给论文加分，也不会因为缺少这个实验而扣分。\n\n现在看到 30% 稀疏度下生成任务的质量成本仍然很明显，这与论文中\"推荐在 30% 运行点\"的表述产生了张力——需要在讨论部分承认困惑度保留并不能完全转化为生成质量的改善。如果 GPU 预算紧张，可以考虑把这部分推迟到附录甚至最终版本。\n\n接下来要评估 RULER 在合成长上下文任务上的表现，包括 4K 和 16K 序列长度，涵盖三种代表性任务类型。\n\n现在看到各种条件下的预期结果数据，包括密集模型、KL 剪枝和 TEAL 方法在不同稀疏度下的性能指标。 Looking at the performance metrics across different context lengths and sparsity levels, I can see the RULER benchmark results showing how retrieval tasks remain most robust—dense models at 4K context achieve near-perfect scores around 96%, while retrieval performance degrades more gracefully with sparsity compared to other capabilities, since the sparsity primarily affects the feed-forward layers rather than the attention mechanisms that drive retrieval.\n\nVariable tracking emerges as the most challenging task type, requiring the model to maintain precision across multiple assignment chains, which explains why even dense models struggle significantly when context extends to 16K—the 4K dense baseline of 88.5% drops substantially, and applying KL-derived sparsity at 30% further reduces performance to 76.3%, indicating that this capability is far more sensitive to both context length and parameter reduction than retrieval tasks.\n\nAt 50% sparsity, variable tracking essentially collapses across both methods, while common words extraction shows more resilience with KL maintaining 82.7% at 4K despite the dense baseline being 91%. The systematic 10-15 percentage point degradation across all metrics when moving from 4K to 16K context reflects the extrapolation gap—the model was only trained on 2048 tokens, so the predictor struggles to generalize to longer sequences—yet KL-derived masks consistently outperform TEAL by roughly the same margin regardless of context length.\n\nLooking at the broader patterns, sparsity impacts FFN-dependent tasks increasingly across the board, with variable tracking being most affected, followed by common words extraction, then needle-in-a-haystack. The KL advantage remains consistent across all task types, hovering around 7-10 percentage points over TEAL at 30% sparsity, though not dramatically larger. This diagnostic value comes from the fine-grained breakdown showing how different task types respond to sparsity and method choice.\n\nThe key question is whether RULER adds unique insights beyond what LongBench would already cover—specifically whether the degradation patterns in reasoning-heavy tasks like variable tracking reveal something fundamental about how sparsity affects state-tracking mechanisms in FFN neurons. If that distinction doesn't emerge clearly, RULER might be better positioned in the appendix to avoid appearing redundant.\n\nI should also verify my LongBench predictions more carefully. Looking at the existing results, the MultiNews score jumping dramatically from 2.5 in the dense setting to 18.01 under KL 30% uniform sparsity is clearly anomalous—likely a metric artifact or experimental bug that needs investigation before drawing conclusions. I'm noticing the MultiNews score jumped dramatically from 2.5 in the dense run to 18.01 in the kl30_uniform run, which seems like a measurement artifact rather than a real improvement. For my median tier predictions, I'll use more realistic baseline scores for Llama-8B models on LongBench tasks—typically around 15-20 for NarrativeQA, 25-35 for Qasper, 25-30 for GovReport, and 20-25 for MultiNews—while acknowledging that the actual dense measurements are considerably lower, possibly due to differences in evaluation framework, few-shot setup, or generation parameters. I'm narrowing down to just the unmeasured conditions since KL 30% uniform and dense baselines are already established. Now I need to double-check my MMLU predictions for the 6-point sweep—at 25% global pruning, I'd be keeping 75% of neurons, so performance should stay very close to the dense baseline since the global top-k removes so few parameters. the importance ranking. Early layers might keep more neurons while later layers prune more aggressively. The real question is whether the 25% predictor's ranking diverges significantly from magnitude-based approaches—at this sparsity level, the overlap should be tighter than at 50%, so the KL predictor's advantage is more modest. My MMLU prediction of around 0.46 at 25% global sparsity aligns with the expected range, and the perplexity estimate of 10.82 matches the narrative guidance for this configuration. I think I have enough to finalize the analysis now.\n\nChecking my predictions against the stated ranges: the C1 uniform eval gap of 0.29 PPL falls within the expected 0.15–0.5 band, the regime R² of ~0.89 sits comfortably in the 0.85–0.95 range, and the KL30 uniform MMLU results with mean 0.385 and std 0.018 align with the target mean of 0.37–0.40 and std of 0.015–0.025. Everything checks out as median tier performance. Time to write the final YAML output with all the predictions organized clearly.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":7996,"cache_read_input_tokens":49874,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":7996},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"19d5cd1b-dd8d-4261-868b-cdcfcf2d032f","uuid":"f4003c41-55b1-4ea1-a32c-19bee2121903"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01Me4HuTvcpW7Zv9nKbcj651","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"```yaml\ntier: median\nnarrative_guidance: >\n  Claim 1 从 \"sole driver\" 调整为 \"primary driver (~85-95% of the gap)\"。C1 uniform\n  eval 显示 BCE+KL-targets PPL 比 KL 高 ~0.29，gradient coupling 贡献 ~8.5%，non-zero\n  but minor。§3.6 从 suggestive intuition 升级为 quantitative decomposition（decompose\n  gap into target quality component 91.5% + gradient coupling component 8.5%）。\n  Claim 3 从 \"phase transition\" 软化为 \"empirical regime structure with noisy boundaries\"，\n  6-point sweep piecewise R² ~0.89，regime 存在但 boundary 在 35-40% 间模糊（±5% tolerance）。\n  BCE 30% forced PPL 10.82 缩小了低 sparsity 下 KL 优势幅度（gap 1.84 vs 50% 下 10.72），\n  但 KL 仍 clearly superior。14B downstream MMLU gap (64.49 vs TEAL 71.83) 是 moderate\n  weakness，rebuttal 需准备额外 2-seed + allocation-sweep 实验计划。\n  LongBench/MT-Bench/RULER 提供 adequate 但非 impressive 的支撑。\n  Reviewer avg ~3.7（range 3.5-4.0），accept probability ~0.35。目标仍 Main 但需\n  Findings fallback plan。\n\nimagined_experiments:\n  - name: \"c1_ablation_eval_uniform\"\n    plan_id: \"c1_ablation_bce_kl_targets\"\n    description: >\n      BCE trained on KL-derived oracle masks 在 TEAL uniform protocol 50% sparsity\n      下评估。Controlled comparison with KL predictor under identical eval protocol。\n    expected_results:\n      bce_kl_targets_uniform_50pct_ppl: 12.25\n      kl_uniform_50pct_ppl_3seed_mean: 11.92\n      ppl_gap: 0.33\n      gradient_coupling_contribution_pct: 8.5\n      gap_as_fraction_of_total_kl_advantage: 0.031\n      bce_kl_targets_uniform_50pct_arc_c: 0.322\n      bce_kl_targets_uniform_50pct_wg: 0.618\n      bce_kl_targets_uniform_50pct_mmlu: 0.271\n      bce_kl_targets_uniform_50pct_gsm8k: 0.017\n      runtime_mask_agreement_with_kl_predictor: 0.86\n    result_rationale: >\n      Median 档核心特征：gradient coupling 有 minor 但可测量的贡献。BCE+KL-targets\n      使用与 KL 完全相同的 oracle masks 训练（agreement 0.978），但 BCE 的 factorized\n      gradient dL/ds_j 仅依赖 neuron j，无法像 KL 的 coupled gradient\n      (dL_KL/ds_j 通过 d_logits/dh × dh/dm 耦合所有 active neurons) 那样在训练中\n      探索 joint interaction patterns。推理时两个 predictor 对大多数 neurons（~85%\n      clearly important/unimportant）做出一致决策，但对 ~15% borderline neurons\n      （activation magnitude 接近 pruning threshold）产生分歧。这些 borderline 分歧\n      累积为 0.33 PPL gap。\n\n      Gap 0.33 远小于 KL vs magnitude-BCE 的总差距（11.92 vs ~22.5 = 10.6 PPL），\n      确认 target quality 是 primary driver (~97% of the KL-vs-BCE gap)。但 gap 非零\n      意味着 gradient coupling 提供了 measurable additional benefit（~3% of gap,\n      or ~8.5% of KL-vs-optimal-BCE advantage）——在 median 档下这足以让 \"sole driver\"\n      claim 不成立。\n\n      Downstream gap 更小因为 50% sparsity 下 MMLU/GSM8K 已接近 random floor\n      （~0.27-0.28），ceiling compression 压缩了差异。\n    tables:\n      - \"Table 3: C1 Causal Ablation — Controlled Comparison (§4.4)\"\n      - \"Table A.3: Gradient Coupling Quantitative Decomposition\"\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Figure 4 panel (c): Uniform protocol bar chart — KL / BCE+KL-targets / magnitude-BCE / TEAL, annotated gap = 0.33\"\n    analysis: >\n      论文叙事需从 \"target quality is the sole driver\" 调整为 \"target quality accounts\n      for ~91-97% of the advantage; gradient coupling provides a minor but non-zero\n      additional benefit (~3-9%)\"。这反而加强了 paper depth：审稿人更信任 nuanced\n      quantitative decomposition（\"we can precisely attribute 91.5% to mask quality\n      and 8.5% to gradient coupling\"）而非 binary claim。§3.6 的理论分析因此获得\n      empirical grounding——coupled gradient 不仅是 mathematical property，而是\n      contributes measurably to borderline neuron decisions。需在 §4.4 新增一段\n      discussion：大多数 neuron importance decisions 在 50% sparsity 下 unambiguous，\n      gradient coupling 的价值集中在 ~10-15% borderline neurons 上。\n\n  - name: \"dense_sparsity_sweep_6pt\"\n    plan_id: \"dense_sparsity_sweep_6pt\"\n    description: >\n      KL predictors at 25%/30%/35%/40%/45%/50%，TEAL global + uniform allocation\n      evaluation。30%/40%/50% 已有实测数据，预测 25%/35%/45% 的 PPL + 4-task downstream。\n      含 regime boundary analysis with piecewise fitting。\n    expected_results:\n      # PPL — TEAL global allocation\n      kl_25pct_teal_global_ppl: 7.92\n      kl_30pct_teal_global_ppl: 8.98\n      kl_35pct_teal_global_ppl: 9.51\n      kl_40pct_teal_global_ppl: 10.12\n      kl_45pct_teal_global_ppl: 10.88\n      kl_50pct_teal_global_ppl: 11.79\n      # PPL — uniform allocation\n      kl_25pct_uniform_ppl: 7.68\n      kl_30pct_uniform_ppl: 8.82\n      kl_35pct_uniform_ppl: 8.47\n      kl_40pct_uniform_ppl: 8.36\n      kl_45pct_uniform_ppl: 9.82\n      kl_50pct_uniform_ppl: 11.96\n      # Downstream — GLOBAL allocation\n      kl_25pct_global_arc_c: 0.461\n      kl_25pct_global_wg: 0.731\n      kl_25pct_global_mmlu: 0.463\n      kl_25pct_global_gsm8k: 0.137\n      kl_35pct_global_arc_c: 0.401\n      kl_35pct_global_wg: 0.679\n      kl_35pct_global_mmlu: 0.312\n      kl_35pct_global_gsm8k: 0.065\n      kl_45pct_global_arc_c: 0.365\n      kl_45pct_global_wg: 0.645\n      kl_45pct_global_mmlu: 0.328\n      kl_45pct_global_gsm8k: 0.034\n      # Downstream — UNIFORM allocation\n      kl_25pct_uniform_arc_c: 0.453\n      kl_25pct_uniform_wg: 0.724\n      kl_25pct_uniform_mmlu: 0.472\n      kl_25pct_uniform_gsm8k: 0.145\n      kl_35pct_uniform_arc_c: 0.415\n      kl_35pct_uniform_wg: 0.683\n      kl_35pct_uniform_mmlu: 0.358\n      kl_35pct_uniform_gsm8k: 0.058\n      kl_45pct_uniform_arc_c: 0.356\n      kl_45pct_uniform_wg: 0.641\n      kl_45pct_uniform_mmlu: 0.293\n      kl_45pct_uniform_gsm8k: 0.028\n      # Regime analysis statistics\n      regime_piecewise_R2_ppl_global: 0.994\n      regime_piecewise_R2_mmlu_global: 0.73\n      regime_piecewise_R2_mmlu_uniform: 0.97\n      regime_piecewise_R2_composite: 0.89\n      regime_boundary_crossover_sparsity: 0.375\n      regime_boundary_tolerance_pp: 5\n      allocation_advantage_25pct: 0.009\n      allocation_advantage_30pct: 0.153\n      allocation_advantage_35pct: 0.046\n      allocation_advantage_40pct: -0.074\n      allocation_advantage_45pct: -0.035\n      allocation_advantage_50pct: -0.001\n    result_rationale: >\n      PPL (TEAL global): 近似线性增长，每 5pp sparsity ≈ +0.75-0.95 PPL，低端增长\n      略慢（25→30%: +1.06）高端略快（45→50%: +0.91）。R² = 0.994 因为 PPL 在\n      global allocation 下由 information-theoretic capacity 主导，是 smooth function\n      of sparsity。25% PPL = 7.92 外推自 30%=8.98，考虑到 25% 下 marginal pruned\n      neurons 的 importance 更低（natural SwiGLU sparsity floor ~5% 意味着最先被\n      pruned 的 5% 几乎是 \"free\"）。\n\n      PPL (uniform): 非单调——35% (8.47) < 30% (8.82)，40% (8.36) < 35% (8.47)。\n      这是因为 uniform allocation 在中等 sparsity 下消除了 layer starvation 同时总\n      capacity 仍然足够。25% uniform (7.68) 甚至 < 25% global (7.92) 因为 global\n      allocation 即使在低 sparsity 也有轻微的 layer imbalance。PPL 非单调性在 median\n      档下是 real effect 但幅度不大（最大 inversion 0.46 PPL）。\n\n      MMLU (global): 高度非单调。25% global = 0.463（接近 dense 0.653 的 71%，因为\n      75% neurons 保留，所有层获得充足 budget）。30% global 急剧降至 0.253（actual）——\n      global top-k 在 30% 时开始显著 starve late knowledge-intensive layers（L24-31\n      retention 仅 50%，L31 仅 28.4%），而 MMLU 高度依赖这些层的 factual recall。\n      35% global = 0.312，略好于 30%——predictor 在 35% target 训练时学会了更均衡的\n      cross-layer priorities。40% global = 0.381（actual），进一步回升因为 tighter\n      budget 下 predictor 的 learned allocation 更有价值，它学会保护 knowledge-critical\n      layers。45% global = 0.328 开始再降因为总 capacity 不足。50% 接近 random 0.279。\n      这种 M-shape 导致 MMLU-global piecewise R² 仅 0.73。\n\n      MMLU (uniform): 单调递减 0.472→0.406→0.358→0.307→0.293→0.278，R² = 0.97。\n      Uniform 消除了 layer starvation artifact，downstream 退化纯粹反映 capacity\n      loss。25% uniform MMLU 0.472 高于 25% global 0.463（margin 小，因为低 sparsity\n      下两种 allocation 差异不大）。\n\n      Allocation crossover: uniform advantage (MMLU_uniform - MMLU_global) 在 25% 为\n      +0.009（negligible），30% 为 +0.153（massive，uniform 显著更优），35% 为 +0.046\n      （uniform 仍优但 gap 缩小），40% 为 -0.074（global 反超），45% 为 -0.035（global\n      微优），50% 为 -0.001（both collapse，irrelevant）。Crossover 在 ~37.5% 处\n      但 boundary 模糊——35% 点仍 uniform > global，40% 点已 global > uniform，\n      exact crossover 在 35-40% 的某处但 6 points 不足以 pinpoint 到 ±2.5% precision。\n      这正是 median 档 \"regime boundaries exist but noisy, ±5% tolerance\" 的体现。\n    tables:\n      - \"Table 5: 6-Point Sparsity Sweep — PPL and Downstream (§4.6)\"\n      - \"Table A.5: Allocation Preference Analysis with Regime Statistics\"\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Figure 5a: PPL vs sparsity (6 points) under global and uniform allocation, dual y-axis\"\n      - \"Figure 5b: MMLU vs sparsity under global (M-shape) vs uniform (monotone), crossover region shaded at 35-40%\"\n      - \"Figure 5c: Allocation advantage heatmap (metric × sparsity), red = uniform better, blue = global better\"\n    analysis: >\n      6-point sweep 在 median 档下确认三个 regime 存在但 boundary 定位模糊。\n      Piecewise composite R² = 0.89——高于 0.85 threshold 说明 regime structure\n      是 real，但低于 0.95 说明不是 clean phase transition。论文需使用 \"empirical\n      regime structure\" 措辞而非 \"phase transition\"，标注 ±5% boundary tolerance。\n      核心发现不变：(1) budget-surplus regime (≤30%) uniform 稳定化效果显著；\n      (2) budget-constrained regime (~35-45%) learned allocation 优于 uniform；\n      (3) capacity-cliff (≥50%) allocation irrelevant。但审稿人可能质疑 \"6 points\n      不足以做 regime analysis\"（需 ≥10 points for convincing piecewise fitting）。\n      Defense: claim 的核心是 qualitative regime existence 而非 precise boundary\n      location，6 points + two allocation strategies 提供了 12 observations。\n\n  - name: \"bce_30pct_forced_topk_eval\"\n    plan_id: \"bce_30pct_forced_topk\"\n    description: >\n      BCE predictor (magnitude oracle targets) 在 30% sparsity 下 TEAL forced top-k\n      评估。填充推荐 operating point 处 BCE baseline。\n    expected_results:\n      bce_30pct_teal_global_ppl: 10.82\n      bce_30pct_arc_c: 0.412\n      bce_30pct_wg: 0.651\n      bce_30pct_mmlu: 0.368\n      bce_30pct_gsm8k: 0.074\n      kl_30pct_teal_global_ppl_ref: 8.98\n      teal_30pct_vanilla_ppl_ref: 10.97\n      ppl_gap_bce_vs_kl: 1.84\n      ppl_gap_bce_vs_teal_vanilla: -0.15\n      relative_gap_bce_vs_kl_pct: 20.5\n    result_rationale: >\n      Median 档 BCE 30% PPL = 10.82 ([10.0, 12.0] range)。\n\n      为什么 10.82 而非更高或更低：(1) 30% sparsity 下 magnitude-based 和 KL-derived\n      masks 的 Jaccard overlap 比 50% 下更高（estimated ~0.55-0.60 at 30% vs 0.40\n      at 50%），因为低 sparsity 下 \"clearly unimportant\" neurons 的 consensus 更大。\n      BCE 的 magnitude oracle targets 在 30% 下的 quality 比 50% 下好很多。\n      (2) 但 BCE predictor 的 factorized training signal 仍然限制了它的 discriminative\n      power——它学到的 importance ranking 本质上是 magnitude ranking 的 smoothed\n      approximation，不发现 KL 那种基于 output distribution 的 joint interaction\n      patterns。(3) BCE 30% PPL (10.82) 非常接近 TEAL 30% vanilla (10.97)，gap 仅\n      0.15 PPL。这说明 BCE learned predictor 在低 sparsity 下几乎不提供超越\n      training-free magnitude pruning 的增益——它本质上 \"学会了 magnitude pruning\"。\n\n      KL 30% (8.98) vs BCE 30% (10.82) gap = 1.84 PPL (20.5% relative)。虽然比\n      50% 下的 gap (10.72 PPL, 91% relative) 小得多，但在 30% 这个推荐 operating\n      point 上 1.84 PPL 仍然 practically significant——对应约 0.04 MMLU improvement\n      和 0.02 downstream gap。\n\n      Downstream：BCE 30% MMLU 0.368 接近 TEAL 30% (0.386) 但低于 KL 30% uniform\n      (0.406)。再次确认 BCE ≈ TEAL in quality，KL clearly better。\n    tables:\n      - \"Table 2: Main Results at 30% Operating Point (§4.3)\"\n      - \"Table A.2: BCE vs KL Gap as Function of Sparsity\"\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Figure 3: Pareto frontier 加入 BCE 30% point, 落在 TEAL baseline cluster 而非 KL curve 上\"\n    analysis: >\n      BCE 30% 数据完善了 paper narrative 的两个关键 argument：(1) KL-derived masks\n      的优势不限于高 sparsity，在推荐 operating point 也显著存在（20.5% relative PPL\n      gap）。(2) BCE predictor ≈ TEAL baseline 进一步支持 \"BCE only learns magnitude\n      pruning\" 的 narrative——learned predictor 的 value 取决于 training signal 的\n      quality，而非 predictor architecture。但 median 档下 1.84 PPL gap 对审稿人\n      可能 \"not dramatic enough\"，尤其是 downstream gap 更小（MMLU 0.368 vs 0.406\n      = +0.038）。需在论文中同时报告 PPL gap 和 downstream gap，强调 \"consistent\n      advantage across all metrics\" 而非 single dramatic number。\n\n  - name: \"plan_010_longbench_full\"\n    plan_id: \"plan_010\"\n    description: >\n      LongBench English subset (4K-32K) 全条件评估。Dense, KL-only (30%/50%),\n      BCE+comp (30%), TEAL (30%/50%), SPON (50%), SPON+KL (50%)。\n      Per-task-type: single-doc QA, multi-doc QA, summarization, few-shot, code。\n    expected_results:\n      # Dense baseline (longbench_dense_v6 actual for measured tasks)\n      dense_narrativeqa_f1: 7.3\n      dense_qasper_f1: 13.4\n      dense_gov_report_rouge: 30.5\n      # KL 30% uniform\n      kl30u_narrativeqa_f1: 4.5\n      kl30u_qasper_f1: 7.2\n      kl30u_gov_report_rouge: 18.4\n      kl30u_avg_retention_vs_dense: 0.58\n      # KL 50% global\n      kl50g_narrativeqa_f1: 2.3\n      kl50g_qasper_f1: 3.6\n      kl50g_gov_report_rouge: 10.2\n      kl50g_avg_retention_vs_dense: 0.31\n      # TEAL 30% baseline\n      teal30_narrativeqa_f1: 3.1\n      teal30_qasper_f1: 5.5\n      teal30_gov_report_rouge: 14.8\n      teal30_avg_retention_vs_dense: 0.46\n      # TEAL 50% baseline\n      teal50_narrativeqa_f1: 1.2\n      teal50_qasper_f1: 2.0\n      teal50_gov_report_rouge: 6.4\n      teal50_avg_retention_vs_dense: 0.19\n      # BCE+comp 30%\n      bce_comp30_narrativeqa_f1: 3.4\n      bce_comp30_qasper_f1: 5.9\n      bce_comp30_gov_report_rouge: 15.5\n      bce_comp30_avg_retention_vs_dense: 0.49\n      # SPON 50%\n      spon50_narrativeqa_f1: 1.4\n      spon50_qasper_f1: 2.3\n      spon50_gov_report_rouge: 7.1\n      # SPON+KL 50%\n      sponkl50_narrativeqa_f1: 2.4\n      sponkl50_qasper_f1: 3.7\n      sponkl50_gov_report_rouge: 10.4\n      # KL advantage over TEAL at 30%\n      kl_vs_teal_30_narrativeqa_delta: 1.4\n      kl_vs_teal_30_qasper_delta: 1.7\n      kl_vs_teal_30_gov_report_delta: 3.6\n    result_rationale: >\n      Long-context evaluation 的退化幅度大于 short-context PPL 暗示的。KL 30% uniform\n      WikiText-2 PPL 仅增加 44%（8.82 vs 6.12），但 NarrativeQA retention 仅 62%\n      （4.5/7.3），GovReport retention 60%（18.4/30.5）。原因：(1) predictor 在 2048\n      tokens 上训练，generalize 到 4K-32K 时对长程 dependency 的 neuron importance\n      估计精度下降——在 training distribution 之外，predictor 可能 prune 掉 long-range\n      attention 所依赖的 FFN neurons。(2) Sparsity 导致每层 residual stream 的\n      information bottleneck 在长序列上 multiplicatively 累积——32 layers × small\n      per-layer information loss = significant end-to-end degradation。(3) QA tasks\n      (NarrativeQA, Qasper) 比 summarization (GovReport) 退化 proportionally 更大\n      因为 QA 需要 precise retrieval + extraction，而 summarization 更依赖 general\n      compression 能力。\n\n      KL 30% consistently outperforms TEAL 30% (NarrativeQA +1.4, Qasper +1.7,\n      GovReport +3.6)。KL advantage 在 long context 下 preserved 甚至 amplified\n      因为 KL-derived masks 更好地保护 cross-layer information flow。\n\n      SPON+KL ≈ KL (NarrativeQA 2.4 vs 2.3, Qasper 3.7 vs 3.6) 确认 Claim 2\n      在 long-context 场景也成立。SPON 的 static bias correction 是 per-layer local\n      adjustment，无法修复 long-range information loss。\n\n      BCE+comp 30% (NarrativeQA 3.4) 介于 TEAL (3.1) 和 KL (4.5) 之间——compensation\n      network 提供 moderate per-layer correction 但无法 substitute KL 的 holistic\n      mask quality。Gap BCE+comp vs KL (1.1 on NarrativeQA) 说明 compensation 的\n      local correction 在 long-context 下价值有限。\n\n      50% sparsity 下所有方法 severe degradation（KL 50% NarrativeQA 仅 2.3，dense\n      的 31%），与 capacity-cliff regime 一致。\n    tables:\n      - \"Table 6: LongBench Results (§4.7)\"\n      - \"Table A.6: LongBench Per-Task-Type Breakdown\"\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Figure 7: LongBench normalized retention radar chart (Dense=1.0 baseline, KL-30%, TEAL-30%, BCE+comp-30%)\"\n    analysis: >\n      LongBench 在 median 档下是 moderate positive。KL consistently outperforms\n      baselines across all task types，但 absolute retention 偏低（30% sparsity 下\n      平均仅 58% of dense performance）。审稿人可能指出 \"even KL 30% loses 42% of\n      long-context quality\"。Defense: (1) predictor 仅在 2048 tokens 训练，long-context\n      generalization 是 zero-shot，这本身 impressive；(2) KL relative advantage over\n      TEAL 在 long context 下 maintained（avg +12pp retention）；(3) long-context\n      quality preservation is orthogonal to this paper's core contribution (mask\n      pattern discovery)，可在 future work 中 address（long-context-aware training）。\n      Median 档下 LongBench 不会成为 paper highlight 但 covers reviewer concern 5。\n\n  - name: \"plan_011_mtbench\"\n    plan_id: \"plan_011\"\n    description: >\n      MT-Bench 80 multi-turn questions, 8 categories, GPT-4o judge。Dense, KL (30%/50%),\n      TEAL (30%/50%), BCE+comp (30%), SPON (50%)。\n    expected_results:\n      dense_mtbench_avg: 6.32\n      kl30_mtbench_avg: 5.18\n      kl50_mtbench_avg: 3.45\n      teal30_mtbench_avg: 4.65\n      teal50_mtbench_avg: 2.82\n      bce_comp30_mtbench_avg: 4.88\n      spon50_mtbench_avg: 2.96\n      # Per-category at KL 30%\n      kl30_writing: 5.85\n      kl30_reasoning: 4.25\n      kl30_math: 3.55\n      kl30_coding: 4.80\n      kl30_extraction: 6.15\n      kl30_stem: 4.50\n      kl30_humanities: 5.65\n      kl30_roleplay: 6.70\n      # Retention vs dense\n      kl30_mtbench_retention: 0.82\n      teal30_mtbench_retention: 0.74\n      kl_vs_teal_30_delta: 0.53\n    result_rationale: >\n      MT-Bench 评估 multi-turn instruction following。Dense Llama-3.1-8B ≈ 6.32\n      （与 community benchmarks 6.2-6.5 一致）。\n\n      KL 30% avg = 5.18（dense 的 82%）。Per-category pattern: extraction (6.15)\n      和 roleplay (6.70) 保留最好因为这些 tasks 主要依赖 pattern matching 和\n      fluency，FFN sparsity 对 surface-level generation quality 影响较小。\n      Reasoning (4.25) 和 math (3.55) 退化最严重因为 multi-step computation 需要\n      precise intermediate representations——pruned neurons 导致 reasoning chain\n      中间步骤的 subtle errors 在 multi-turn 对话中累积。Writing (5.85) 和\n      humanities (5.65) 中等退化——需要 knowledge recall (affected by sparsity)\n      但不需要 precise computation chains。\n\n      KL 50% avg = 3.45（dense 的 55%）：capacity-cliff 下 multi-turn 对话质量\n      catastrophic——second turn 通常 incoherent 因为 model 需要同时 maintain\n      first-turn context 和 generate follow-up，在 sparse regime 下 attention +\n      sparse FFN 的 double bottleneck 导致 information loss。\n\n      KL 30% vs TEAL 30% delta = 0.53（5.18 vs 4.65），relative improvement 11.4%。\n      Gap 比 PPL space 小因为 MT-Bench 是 1-10 discrete scale，GPT-4o judge 对\n      minor quality differences 不太敏感（quantization effect）。BCE+comp 30% = 4.88\n      介于 TEAL 和 KL 之间——compensation network 在 generation quality 上的增益\n      有限，与 LongBench 一致。\n    tables:\n      - \"Table 7: MT-Bench Multi-Turn Evaluation (§4.8 or Appendix)\"\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Figure A.3: MT-Bench per-category grouped bar chart (Dense / KL-30% / TEAL-30%)\"\n    analysis: >\n      MT-Bench 是 P2 实验，median 档下不改变核心叙事。主要价值：(1) 证明 KL advantage\n      extends to open-ended generation；(2) per-category breakdown 提供 insight into\n      which capabilities are most affected by sparsity (reasoning > knowledge > fluency)。\n      但 absolute degradation 显著（dense 6.32 → KL 30% 5.18, -18%），与 paper framing\n      的 \"recommended operating point at 30%\" 形成 tension。需在 discussion 中\n      acknowledge: \"PPL preservation (44% increase) understates generation quality\n      degradation (18% MT-Bench drop), as PPL captures average-case perplexity while\n      MT-Bench targets worst-case reasoning chains。\" 如 GPU 预算紧张或提交前时间\n      不足，MT-Bench 可 defer 到 appendix 或 camera-ready。在 median 档下审稿人\n      不会因为缺少 MT-Bench 而 reject，但有则加分。\n\n  - name: \"plan_012_ruler\"\n    plan_id: \"plan_012\"\n    description: >\n      RULER synthetic long-context at 4K and 16K sequence lengths。3 task types:\n      NIAH (Needle-in-a-Haystack), VT (Variable Tracking), CWE (Common Words\n      Extraction)。Dense, KL (30%/50%), TEAL (30%/50%)。\n    expected_results:\n      # 4K context\n      dense_4k_niah_acc: 96.2\n      dense_4k_vt_acc: 87.5\n      dense_4k_cwe_acc: 91.0\n      kl30_4k_niah_acc: 88.8\n      kl30_4k_vt_acc: 75.2\n      kl30_4k_cwe_acc: 82.4\n      kl50_4k_niah_acc: 67.5\n      kl50_4k_vt_acc: 51.8\n      kl50_4k_cwe_acc: 59.3\n      teal30_4k_niah_acc: 81.6\n      teal30_4k_vt_acc: 67.8\n      teal30_4k_cwe_acc: 73.5\n      teal50_4k_niah_acc: 50.2\n      teal50_4k_vt_acc: 37.9\n      teal50_4k_cwe_acc: 41.8\n      # 16K context\n      dense_16k_niah_acc: 91.5\n      dense_16k_vt_acc: 73.8\n      dense_16k_cwe_acc: 82.1\n      kl30_16k_niah_acc: 77.9\n      kl30_16k_vt_acc: 57.4\n      kl30_16k_cwe_acc: 67.8\n      kl50_16k_niah_acc: 47.6\n      kl50_16k_vt_acc: 33.1\n      kl50_16k_cwe_acc: 41.2\n      teal30_16k_niah_acc: 67.8\n      teal30_16k_vt_acc: 47.5\n      teal30_16k_cwe_acc: 58.3\n      teal50_16k_niah_acc: 34.5\n      teal50_16k_vt_acc: 23.8\n      teal50_16k_cwe_acc: 30.2\n      # Summary\n      kl_vs_teal_30_avg_advantage_4k: 8.3\n      kl_vs_teal_30_avg_advantage_16k: 9.8\n      task_difficulty_ranking: \"VT > CWE > NIAH (most to least affected by sparsity)\"\n    result_rationale: >\n      RULER 提供 controlled synthetic diagnostics，消除 natural language evaluation\n      的 noise。\n\n      NIAH (retrieval): 最 robust。Dense 4K ≈ 96%（near-perfect）。KL 30% 降至 89%\n      （-7pp），因为 retrieval 主要依赖 attention heads 的 query-key matching，FFN\n      sparsity 仅间接影响 residual stream quality。TEAL 30% (82%) 比 KL 30% (89%)\n      差 7pp，说明 KL masks 更好保护 attention-critical FFN neurons（那些 encode\n      position-dependent information 的 neurons）。16K 下 dense 已降至 92%，KL 30%\n      降至 78%——additional 11pp drop vs 4K 反映 predictor 从 2048 training length 到\n      16K 的 extrapolation degradation（对长程 dependency 的 neuron importance 估计\n      不准确）。\n\n      VT (variable tracking): 最 sensitive。Dense 4K = 88%（本身不完美因为 Llama-8B\n      的 multi-hop reasoning 有限）。KL 30% 4K = 75%（-13pp），degradation 比 NIAH\n      大因为 variable tracking 需要 precise intermediate computation——每层 FFN 的\n      pruned neurons 导致 variable state 的 subtle corruption 在多步 tracking 中\n      累积。KL 50% 4K VT = 52%——接近 random baseline，与 capacity-cliff 一致。\n      16K 进一步降至 KL 30% = 57%，因为更长 context 意味着更多 variable assignment\n      steps，amplifying per-step errors。\n\n      CWE (common words extraction): 中等。需要 global context understanding\n      但不需要 precise multi-hop reasoning。退化模式介于 NIAH 和 VT 之间。\n\n      KL vs TEAL advantage 在 16K 下略微扩大（+9.8pp vs +8.3pp at 4K），说明\n      KL-derived masks 的 information preservation advantage 在长序列下更重要——\n      更多 layers × longer sequences = 更大的 cumulative benefit from better masks。\n      但 median 档下这个 trend 不 dramatic enough to be a standalone finding。\n    tables:\n      - \"Table A.7: RULER Results at 4K and 16K (Appendix)\"\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Figure A.4: RULER accuracy heatmap (rows = method, columns = task_type × context_length), color = accuracy\"\n    analysis: >\n      RULER 在 median 档下是 Appendix material，补充 plan_010 LongBench。主要 unique\n      value: (1) task-type decomposition 明确了 sparsity 对不同 capability 的\n      differential impact（VT > CWE > NIAH），提供 mechanistic insight \"FFN sparsity\n      disproportionately hurts state-tracking and multi-hop reasoning\"。(2) 4K vs\n      16K comparison 量化了 predictor 的 length generalization gap。但如果 plan_010\n      已 cover long-context evaluation，审稿人可能视 RULER 为 redundant。建议：\n      仅在 RULER 的 VT 退化 pattern 产生了 LongBench 未 reveal 的 insight 时放\n      正文，否则 appendix。Median 档下 RULER 不太可能影响 reviewer score。\n```"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":7996,"cache_read_input_tokens":49874,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":7996},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"19d5cd1b-dd8d-4261-868b-cdcfcf2d032f","uuid":"e32d4c13-5044-4867-99df-46838c6cfa34"}
{"type":"result","subtype":"success","is_error":false,"api_error_status":null,"duration_ms":618748,"duration_api_ms":621236,"num_turns":3,"result":"```yaml\ntier: median\nnarrative_guidance: >\n  Claim 1 从 \"sole driver\" 调整为 \"primary driver (~85-95% of the gap)\"。C1 uniform\n  eval 显示 BCE+KL-targets PPL 比 KL 高 ~0.29，gradient coupling 贡献 ~8.5%，non-zero\n  but minor。§3.6 从 suggestive intuition 升级为 quantitative decomposition（decompose\n  gap into target quality component 91.5% + gradient coupling component 8.5%）。\n  Claim 3 从 \"phase transition\" 软化为 \"empirical regime structure with noisy boundaries\"，\n  6-point sweep piecewise R² ~0.89，regime 存在但 boundary 在 35-40% 间模糊（±5% tolerance）。\n  BCE 30% forced PPL 10.82 缩小了低 sparsity 下 KL 优势幅度（gap 1.84 vs 50% 下 10.72），\n  但 KL 仍 clearly superior。14B downstream MMLU gap (64.49 vs TEAL 71.83) 是 moderate\n  weakness，rebuttal 需准备额外 2-seed + allocation-sweep 实验计划。\n  LongBench/MT-Bench/RULER 提供 adequate 但非 impressive 的支撑。\n  Reviewer avg ~3.7（range 3.5-4.0），accept probability ~0.35。目标仍 Main 但需\n  Findings fallback plan。\n\nimagined_experiments:\n  - name: \"c1_ablation_eval_uniform\"\n    plan_id: \"c1_ablation_bce_kl_targets\"\n    description: >\n      BCE trained on KL-derived oracle masks 在 TEAL uniform protocol 50% sparsity\n      下评估。Controlled comparison with KL predictor under identical eval protocol。\n    expected_results:\n      bce_kl_targets_uniform_50pct_ppl: 12.25\n      kl_uniform_50pct_ppl_3seed_mean: 11.92\n      ppl_gap: 0.33\n      gradient_coupling_contribution_pct: 8.5\n      gap_as_fraction_of_total_kl_advantage: 0.031\n      bce_kl_targets_uniform_50pct_arc_c: 0.322\n      bce_kl_targets_uniform_50pct_wg: 0.618\n      bce_kl_targets_uniform_50pct_mmlu: 0.271\n      bce_kl_targets_uniform_50pct_gsm8k: 0.017\n      runtime_mask_agreement_with_kl_predictor: 0.86\n    result_rationale: >\n      Median 档核心特征：gradient coupling 有 minor 但可测量的贡献。BCE+KL-targets\n      使用与 KL 完全相同的 oracle masks 训练（agreement 0.978），但 BCE 的 factorized\n      gradient dL/ds_j 仅依赖 neuron j，无法像 KL 的 coupled gradient\n      (dL_KL/ds_j 通过 d_logits/dh × dh/dm 耦合所有 active neurons) 那样在训练中\n      探索 joint interaction patterns。推理时两个 predictor 对大多数 neurons（~85%\n      clearly important/unimportant）做出一致决策，但对 ~15% borderline neurons\n      （activation magnitude 接近 pruning threshold）产生分歧。这些 borderline 分歧\n      累积为 0.33 PPL gap。\n\n      Gap 0.33 远小于 KL vs magnitude-BCE 的总差距（11.92 vs ~22.5 = 10.6 PPL），\n      确认 target quality 是 primary driver (~97% of the KL-vs-BCE gap)。但 gap 非零\n      意味着 gradient coupling 提供了 measurable additional benefit（~3% of gap,\n      or ~8.5% of KL-vs-optimal-BCE advantage）——在 median 档下这足以让 \"sole driver\"\n      claim 不成立。\n\n      Downstream gap 更小因为 50% sparsity 下 MMLU/GSM8K 已接近 random floor\n      （~0.27-0.28），ceiling compression 压缩了差异。\n    tables:\n      - \"Table 3: C1 Causal Ablation — Controlled Comparison (§4.4)\"\n      - \"Table A.3: Gradient Coupling Quantitative Decomposition\"\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Figure 4 panel (c): Uniform protocol bar chart — KL / BCE+KL-targets / magnitude-BCE / TEAL, annotated gap = 0.33\"\n    analysis: >\n      论文叙事需从 \"target quality is the sole driver\" 调整为 \"target quality accounts\n      for ~91-97% of the advantage; gradient coupling provides a minor but non-zero\n      additional benefit (~3-9%)\"。这反而加强了 paper depth：审稿人更信任 nuanced\n      quantitative decomposition（\"we can precisely attribute 91.5% to mask quality\n      and 8.5% to gradient coupling\"）而非 binary claim。§3.6 的理论分析因此获得\n      empirical grounding——coupled gradient 不仅是 mathematical property，而是\n      contributes measurably to borderline neuron decisions。需在 §4.4 新增一段\n      discussion：大多数 neuron importance decisions 在 50% sparsity 下 unambiguous，\n      gradient coupling 的价值集中在 ~10-15% borderline neurons 上。\n\n  - name: \"dense_sparsity_sweep_6pt\"\n    plan_id: \"dense_sparsity_sweep_6pt\"\n    description: >\n      KL predictors at 25%/30%/35%/40%/45%/50%，TEAL global + uniform allocation\n      evaluation。30%/40%/50% 已有实测数据，预测 25%/35%/45% 的 PPL + 4-task downstream。\n      含 regime boundary analysis with piecewise fitting。\n    expected_results:\n      # PPL — TEAL global allocation\n      kl_25pct_teal_global_ppl: 7.92\n      kl_30pct_teal_global_ppl: 8.98\n      kl_35pct_teal_global_ppl: 9.51\n      kl_40pct_teal_global_ppl: 10.12\n      kl_45pct_teal_global_ppl: 10.88\n      kl_50pct_teal_global_ppl: 11.79\n      # PPL — uniform allocation\n      kl_25pct_uniform_ppl: 7.68\n      kl_30pct_uniform_ppl: 8.82\n      kl_35pct_uniform_ppl: 8.47\n      kl_40pct_uniform_ppl: 8.36\n      kl_45pct_uniform_ppl: 9.82\n      kl_50pct_uniform_ppl: 11.96\n      # Downstream — GLOBAL allocation\n      kl_25pct_global_arc_c: 0.461\n      kl_25pct_global_wg: 0.731\n      kl_25pct_global_mmlu: 0.463\n      kl_25pct_global_gsm8k: 0.137\n      kl_35pct_global_arc_c: 0.401\n      kl_35pct_global_wg: 0.679\n      kl_35pct_global_mmlu: 0.312\n      kl_35pct_global_gsm8k: 0.065\n      kl_45pct_global_arc_c: 0.365\n      kl_45pct_global_wg: 0.645\n      kl_45pct_global_mmlu: 0.328\n      kl_45pct_global_gsm8k: 0.034\n      # Downstream — UNIFORM allocation\n      kl_25pct_uniform_arc_c: 0.453\n      kl_25pct_uniform_wg: 0.724\n      kl_25pct_uniform_mmlu: 0.472\n      kl_25pct_uniform_gsm8k: 0.145\n      kl_35pct_uniform_arc_c: 0.415\n      kl_35pct_uniform_wg: 0.683\n      kl_35pct_uniform_mmlu: 0.358\n      kl_35pct_uniform_gsm8k: 0.058\n      kl_45pct_uniform_arc_c: 0.356\n      kl_45pct_uniform_wg: 0.641\n      kl_45pct_uniform_mmlu: 0.293\n      kl_45pct_uniform_gsm8k: 0.028\n      # Regime analysis statistics\n      regime_piecewise_R2_ppl_global: 0.994\n      regime_piecewise_R2_mmlu_global: 0.73\n      regime_piecewise_R2_mmlu_uniform: 0.97\n      regime_piecewise_R2_composite: 0.89\n      regime_boundary_crossover_sparsity: 0.375\n      regime_boundary_tolerance_pp: 5\n      allocation_advantage_25pct: 0.009\n      allocation_advantage_30pct: 0.153\n      allocation_advantage_35pct: 0.046\n      allocation_advantage_40pct: -0.074\n      allocation_advantage_45pct: -0.035\n      allocation_advantage_50pct: -0.001\n    result_rationale: >\n      PPL (TEAL global): 近似线性增长，每 5pp sparsity ≈ +0.75-0.95 PPL，低端增长\n      略慢（25→30%: +1.06）高端略快（45→50%: +0.91）。R² = 0.994 因为 PPL 在\n      global allocation 下由 information-theoretic capacity 主导，是 smooth function\n      of sparsity。25% PPL = 7.92 外推自 30%=8.98，考虑到 25% 下 marginal pruned\n      neurons 的 importance 更低（natural SwiGLU sparsity floor ~5% 意味着最先被\n      pruned 的 5% 几乎是 \"free\"）。\n\n      PPL (uniform): 非单调——35% (8.47) < 30% (8.82)，40% (8.36) < 35% (8.47)。\n      这是因为 uniform allocation 在中等 sparsity 下消除了 layer starvation 同时总\n      capacity 仍然足够。25% uniform (7.68) 甚至 < 25% global (7.92) 因为 global\n      allocation 即使在低 sparsity 也有轻微的 layer imbalance。PPL 非单调性在 median\n      档下是 real effect 但幅度不大（最大 inversion 0.46 PPL）。\n\n      MMLU (global): 高度非单调。25% global = 0.463（接近 dense 0.653 的 71%，因为\n      75% neurons 保留，所有层获得充足 budget）。30% global 急剧降至 0.253（actual）——\n      global top-k 在 30% 时开始显著 starve late knowledge-intensive layers（L24-31\n      retention 仅 50%，L31 仅 28.4%），而 MMLU 高度依赖这些层的 factual recall。\n      35% global = 0.312，略好于 30%——predictor 在 35% target 训练时学会了更均衡的\n      cross-layer priorities。40% global = 0.381（actual），进一步回升因为 tighter\n      budget 下 predictor 的 learned allocation 更有价值，它学会保护 knowledge-critical\n      layers。45% global = 0.328 开始再降因为总 capacity 不足。50% 接近 random 0.279。\n      这种 M-shape 导致 MMLU-global piecewise R² 仅 0.73。\n\n      MMLU (uniform): 单调递减 0.472→0.406→0.358→0.307→0.293→0.278，R² = 0.97。\n      Uniform 消除了 layer starvation artifact，downstream 退化纯粹反映 capacity\n      loss。25% uniform MMLU 0.472 高于 25% global 0.463（margin 小，因为低 sparsity\n      下两种 allocation 差异不大）。\n\n      Allocation crossover: uniform advantage (MMLU_uniform - MMLU_global) 在 25% 为\n      +0.009（negligible），30% 为 +0.153（massive，uniform 显著更优），35% 为 +0.046\n      （uniform 仍优但 gap 缩小），40% 为 -0.074（global 反超），45% 为 -0.035（global\n      微优），50% 为 -0.001（both collapse，irrelevant）。Crossover 在 ~37.5% 处\n      但 boundary 模糊——35% 点仍 uniform > global，40% 点已 global > uniform，\n      exact crossover 在 35-40% 的某处但 6 points 不足以 pinpoint 到 ±2.5% precision。\n      这正是 median 档 \"regime boundaries exist but noisy, ±5% tolerance\" 的体现。\n    tables:\n      - \"Table 5: 6-Point Sparsity Sweep — PPL and Downstream (§4.6)\"\n      - \"Table A.5: Allocation Preference Analysis with Regime Statistics\"\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Figure 5a: PPL vs sparsity (6 points) under global and uniform allocation, dual y-axis\"\n      - \"Figure 5b: MMLU vs sparsity under global (M-shape) vs uniform (monotone), crossover region shaded at 35-40%\"\n      - \"Figure 5c: Allocation advantage heatmap (metric × sparsity), red = uniform better, blue = global better\"\n    analysis: >\n      6-point sweep 在 median 档下确认三个 regime 存在但 boundary 定位模糊。\n      Piecewise composite R² = 0.89——高于 0.85 threshold 说明 regime structure\n      是 real，但低于 0.95 说明不是 clean phase transition。论文需使用 \"empirical\n      regime structure\" 措辞而非 \"phase transition\"，标注 ±5% boundary tolerance。\n      核心发现不变：(1) budget-surplus regime (≤30%) uniform 稳定化效果显著；\n      (2) budget-constrained regime (~35-45%) learned allocation 优于 uniform；\n      (3) capacity-cliff (≥50%) allocation irrelevant。但审稿人可能质疑 \"6 points\n      不足以做 regime analysis\"（需 ≥10 points for convincing piecewise fitting）。\n      Defense: claim 的核心是 qualitative regime existence 而非 precise boundary\n      location，6 points + two allocation strategies 提供了 12 observations。\n\n  - name: \"bce_30pct_forced_topk_eval\"\n    plan_id: \"bce_30pct_forced_topk\"\n    description: >\n      BCE predictor (magnitude oracle targets) 在 30% sparsity 下 TEAL forced top-k\n      评估。填充推荐 operating point 处 BCE baseline。\n    expected_results:\n      bce_30pct_teal_global_ppl: 10.82\n      bce_30pct_arc_c: 0.412\n      bce_30pct_wg: 0.651\n      bce_30pct_mmlu: 0.368\n      bce_30pct_gsm8k: 0.074\n      kl_30pct_teal_global_ppl_ref: 8.98\n      teal_30pct_vanilla_ppl_ref: 10.97\n      ppl_gap_bce_vs_kl: 1.84\n      ppl_gap_bce_vs_teal_vanilla: -0.15\n      relative_gap_bce_vs_kl_pct: 20.5\n    result_rationale: >\n      Median 档 BCE 30% PPL = 10.82 ([10.0, 12.0] range)。\n\n      为什么 10.82 而非更高或更低：(1) 30% sparsity 下 magnitude-based 和 KL-derived\n      masks 的 Jaccard overlap 比 50% 下更高（estimated ~0.55-0.60 at 30% vs 0.40\n      at 50%），因为低 sparsity 下 \"clearly unimportant\" neurons 的 consensus 更大。\n      BCE 的 magnitude oracle targets 在 30% 下的 quality 比 50% 下好很多。\n      (2) 但 BCE predictor 的 factorized training signal 仍然限制了它的 discriminative\n      power——它学到的 importance ranking 本质上是 magnitude ranking 的 smoothed\n      approximation，不发现 KL 那种基于 output distribution 的 joint interaction\n      patterns。(3) BCE 30% PPL (10.82) 非常接近 TEAL 30% vanilla (10.97)，gap 仅\n      0.15 PPL。这说明 BCE learned predictor 在低 sparsity 下几乎不提供超越\n      training-free magnitude pruning 的增益——它本质上 \"学会了 magnitude pruning\"。\n\n      KL 30% (8.98) vs BCE 30% (10.82) gap = 1.84 PPL (20.5% relative)。虽然比\n      50% 下的 gap (10.72 PPL, 91% relative) 小得多，但在 30% 这个推荐 operating\n      point 上 1.84 PPL 仍然 practically significant——对应约 0.04 MMLU improvement\n      和 0.02 downstream gap。\n\n      Downstream：BCE 30% MMLU 0.368 接近 TEAL 30% (0.386) 但低于 KL 30% uniform\n      (0.406)。再次确认 BCE ≈ TEAL in quality，KL clearly better。\n    tables:\n      - \"Table 2: Main Results at 30% Operating Point (§4.3)\"\n      - \"Table A.2: BCE vs KL Gap as Function of Sparsity\"\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Figure 3: Pareto frontier 加入 BCE 30% point, 落在 TEAL baseline cluster 而非 KL curve 上\"\n    analysis: >\n      BCE 30% 数据完善了 paper narrative 的两个关键 argument：(1) KL-derived masks\n      的优势不限于高 sparsity，在推荐 operating point 也显著存在（20.5% relative PPL\n      gap）。(2) BCE predictor ≈ TEAL baseline 进一步支持 \"BCE only learns magnitude\n      pruning\" 的 narrative——learned predictor 的 value 取决于 training signal 的\n      quality，而非 predictor architecture。但 median 档下 1.84 PPL gap 对审稿人\n      可能 \"not dramatic enough\"，尤其是 downstream gap 更小（MMLU 0.368 vs 0.406\n      = +0.038）。需在论文中同时报告 PPL gap 和 downstream gap，强调 \"consistent\n      advantage across all metrics\" 而非 single dramatic number。\n\n  - name: \"plan_010_longbench_full\"\n    plan_id: \"plan_010\"\n    description: >\n      LongBench English subset (4K-32K) 全条件评估。Dense, KL-only (30%/50%),\n      BCE+comp (30%), TEAL (30%/50%), SPON (50%), SPON+KL (50%)。\n      Per-task-type: single-doc QA, multi-doc QA, summarization, few-shot, code。\n    expected_results:\n      # Dense baseline (longbench_dense_v6 actual for measured tasks)\n      dense_narrativeqa_f1: 7.3\n      dense_qasper_f1: 13.4\n      dense_gov_report_rouge: 30.5\n      # KL 30% uniform\n      kl30u_narrativeqa_f1: 4.5\n      kl30u_qasper_f1: 7.2\n      kl30u_gov_report_rouge: 18.4\n      kl30u_avg_retention_vs_dense: 0.58\n      # KL 50% global\n      kl50g_narrativeqa_f1: 2.3\n      kl50g_qasper_f1: 3.6\n      kl50g_gov_report_rouge: 10.2\n      kl50g_avg_retention_vs_dense: 0.31\n      # TEAL 30% baseline\n      teal30_narrativeqa_f1: 3.1\n      teal30_qasper_f1: 5.5\n      teal30_gov_report_rouge: 14.8\n      teal30_avg_retention_vs_dense: 0.46\n      # TEAL 50% baseline\n      teal50_narrativeqa_f1: 1.2\n      teal50_qasper_f1: 2.0\n      teal50_gov_report_rouge: 6.4\n      teal50_avg_retention_vs_dense: 0.19\n      # BCE+comp 30%\n      bce_comp30_narrativeqa_f1: 3.4\n      bce_comp30_qasper_f1: 5.9\n      bce_comp30_gov_report_rouge: 15.5\n      bce_comp30_avg_retention_vs_dense: 0.49\n      # SPON 50%\n      spon50_narrativeqa_f1: 1.4\n      spon50_qasper_f1: 2.3\n      spon50_gov_report_rouge: 7.1\n      # SPON+KL 50%\n      sponkl50_narrativeqa_f1: 2.4\n      sponkl50_qasper_f1: 3.7\n      sponkl50_gov_report_rouge: 10.4\n      # KL advantage over TEAL at 30%\n      kl_vs_teal_30_narrativeqa_delta: 1.4\n      kl_vs_teal_30_qasper_delta: 1.7\n      kl_vs_teal_30_gov_report_delta: 3.6\n    result_rationale: >\n      Long-context evaluation 的退化幅度大于 short-context PPL 暗示的。KL 30% uniform\n      WikiText-2 PPL 仅增加 44%（8.82 vs 6.12），但 NarrativeQA retention 仅 62%\n      （4.5/7.3），GovReport retention 60%（18.4/30.5）。原因：(1) predictor 在 2048\n      tokens 上训练，generalize 到 4K-32K 时对长程 dependency 的 neuron importance\n      估计精度下降——在 training distribution 之外，predictor 可能 prune 掉 long-range\n      attention 所依赖的 FFN neurons。(2) Sparsity 导致每层 residual stream 的\n      information bottleneck 在长序列上 multiplicatively 累积——32 layers × small\n      per-layer information loss = significant end-to-end degradation。(3) QA tasks\n      (NarrativeQA, Qasper) 比 summarization (GovReport) 退化 proportionally 更大\n      因为 QA 需要 precise retrieval + extraction，而 summarization 更依赖 general\n      compression 能力。\n\n      KL 30% consistently outperforms TEAL 30% (NarrativeQA +1.4, Qasper +1.7,\n      GovReport +3.6)。KL advantage 在 long context 下 preserved 甚至 amplified\n      因为 KL-derived masks 更好地保护 cross-layer information flow。\n\n      SPON+KL ≈ KL (NarrativeQA 2.4 vs 2.3, Qasper 3.7 vs 3.6) 确认 Claim 2\n      在 long-context 场景也成立。SPON 的 static bias correction 是 per-layer local\n      adjustment，无法修复 long-range information loss。\n\n      BCE+comp 30% (NarrativeQA 3.4) 介于 TEAL (3.1) 和 KL (4.5) 之间——compensation\n      network 提供 moderate per-layer correction 但无法 substitute KL 的 holistic\n      mask quality。Gap BCE+comp vs KL (1.1 on NarrativeQA) 说明 compensation 的\n      local correction 在 long-context 下价值有限。\n\n      50% sparsity 下所有方法 severe degradation（KL 50% NarrativeQA 仅 2.3，dense\n      的 31%），与 capacity-cliff regime 一致。\n    tables:\n      - \"Table 6: LongBench Results (§4.7)\"\n      - \"Table A.6: LongBench Per-Task-Type Breakdown\"\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Figure 7: LongBench normalized retention radar chart (Dense=1.0 baseline, KL-30%, TEAL-30%, BCE+comp-30%)\"\n    analysis: >\n      LongBench 在 median 档下是 moderate positive。KL consistently outperforms\n      baselines across all task types，但 absolute retention 偏低（30% sparsity 下\n      平均仅 58% of dense performance）。审稿人可能指出 \"even KL 30% loses 42% of\n      long-context quality\"。Defense: (1) predictor 仅在 2048 tokens 训练，long-context\n      generalization 是 zero-shot，这本身 impressive；(2) KL relative advantage over\n      TEAL 在 long context 下 maintained（avg +12pp retention）；(3) long-context\n      quality preservation is orthogonal to this paper's core contribution (mask\n      pattern discovery)，可在 future work 中 address（long-context-aware training）。\n      Median 档下 LongBench 不会成为 paper highlight 但 covers reviewer concern 5。\n\n  - name: \"plan_011_mtbench\"\n    plan_id: \"plan_011\"\n    description: >\n      MT-Bench 80 multi-turn questions, 8 categories, GPT-4o judge。Dense, KL (30%/50%),\n      TEAL (30%/50%), BCE+comp (30%), SPON (50%)。\n    expected_results:\n      dense_mtbench_avg: 6.32\n      kl30_mtbench_avg: 5.18\n      kl50_mtbench_avg: 3.45\n      teal30_mtbench_avg: 4.65\n      teal50_mtbench_avg: 2.82\n      bce_comp30_mtbench_avg: 4.88\n      spon50_mtbench_avg: 2.96\n      # Per-category at KL 30%\n      kl30_writing: 5.85\n      kl30_reasoning: 4.25\n      kl30_math: 3.55\n      kl30_coding: 4.80\n      kl30_extraction: 6.15\n      kl30_stem: 4.50\n      kl30_humanities: 5.65\n      kl30_roleplay: 6.70\n      # Retention vs dense\n      kl30_mtbench_retention: 0.82\n      teal30_mtbench_retention: 0.74\n      kl_vs_teal_30_delta: 0.53\n    result_rationale: >\n      MT-Bench 评估 multi-turn instruction following。Dense Llama-3.1-8B ≈ 6.32\n      （与 community benchmarks 6.2-6.5 一致）。\n\n      KL 30% avg = 5.18（dense 的 82%）。Per-category pattern: extraction (6.15)\n      和 roleplay (6.70) 保留最好因为这些 tasks 主要依赖 pattern matching 和\n      fluency，FFN sparsity 对 surface-level generation quality 影响较小。\n      Reasoning (4.25) 和 math (3.55) 退化最严重因为 multi-step computation 需要\n      precise intermediate representations——pruned neurons 导致 reasoning chain\n      中间步骤的 subtle errors 在 multi-turn 对话中累积。Writing (5.85) 和\n      humanities (5.65) 中等退化——需要 knowledge recall (affected by sparsity)\n      但不需要 precise computation chains。\n\n      KL 50% avg = 3.45（dense 的 55%）：capacity-cliff 下 multi-turn 对话质量\n      catastrophic——second turn 通常 incoherent 因为 model 需要同时 maintain\n      first-turn context 和 generate follow-up，在 sparse regime 下 attention +\n      sparse FFN 的 double bottleneck 导致 information loss。\n\n      KL 30% vs TEAL 30% delta = 0.53（5.18 vs 4.65），relative improvement 11.4%。\n      Gap 比 PPL space 小因为 MT-Bench 是 1-10 discrete scale，GPT-4o judge 对\n      minor quality differences 不太敏感（quantization effect）。BCE+comp 30% = 4.88\n      介于 TEAL 和 KL 之间——compensation network 在 generation quality 上的增益\n      有限，与 LongBench 一致。\n    tables:\n      - \"Table 7: MT-Bench Multi-Turn Evaluation (§4.8 or Appendix)\"\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Figure A.3: MT-Bench per-category grouped bar chart (Dense / KL-30% / TEAL-30%)\"\n    analysis: >\n      MT-Bench 是 P2 实验，median 档下不改变核心叙事。主要价值：(1) 证明 KL advantage\n      extends to open-ended generation；(2) per-category breakdown 提供 insight into\n      which capabilities are most affected by sparsity (reasoning > knowledge > fluency)。\n      但 absolute degradation 显著（dense 6.32 → KL 30% 5.18, -18%），与 paper framing\n      的 \"recommended operating point at 30%\" 形成 tension。需在 discussion 中\n      acknowledge: \"PPL preservation (44% increase) understates generation quality\n      degradation (18% MT-Bench drop), as PPL captures average-case perplexity while\n      MT-Bench targets worst-case reasoning chains。\" 如 GPU 预算紧张或提交前时间\n      不足，MT-Bench 可 defer 到 appendix 或 camera-ready。在 median 档下审稿人\n      不会因为缺少 MT-Bench 而 reject，但有则加分。\n\n  - name: \"plan_012_ruler\"\n    plan_id: \"plan_012\"\n    description: >\n      RULER synthetic long-context at 4K and 16K sequence lengths。3 task types:\n      NIAH (Needle-in-a-Haystack), VT (Variable Tracking), CWE (Common Words\n      Extraction)。Dense, KL (30%/50%), TEAL (30%/50%)。\n    expected_results:\n      # 4K context\n      dense_4k_niah_acc: 96.2\n      dense_4k_vt_acc: 87.5\n      dense_4k_cwe_acc: 91.0\n      kl30_4k_niah_acc: 88.8\n      kl30_4k_vt_acc: 75.2\n      kl30_4k_cwe_acc: 82.4\n      kl50_4k_niah_acc: 67.5\n      kl50_4k_vt_acc: 51.8\n      kl50_4k_cwe_acc: 59.3\n      teal30_4k_niah_acc: 81.6\n      teal30_4k_vt_acc: 67.8\n      teal30_4k_cwe_acc: 73.5\n      teal50_4k_niah_acc: 50.2\n      teal50_4k_vt_acc: 37.9\n      teal50_4k_cwe_acc: 41.8\n      # 16K context\n      dense_16k_niah_acc: 91.5\n      dense_16k_vt_acc: 73.8\n      dense_16k_cwe_acc: 82.1\n      kl30_16k_niah_acc: 77.9\n      kl30_16k_vt_acc: 57.4\n      kl30_16k_cwe_acc: 67.8\n      kl50_16k_niah_acc: 47.6\n      kl50_16k_vt_acc: 33.1\n      kl50_16k_cwe_acc: 41.2\n      teal30_16k_niah_acc: 67.8\n      teal30_16k_vt_acc: 47.5\n      teal30_16k_cwe_acc: 58.3\n      teal50_16k_niah_acc: 34.5\n      teal50_16k_vt_acc: 23.8\n      teal50_16k_cwe_acc: 30.2\n      # Summary\n      kl_vs_teal_30_avg_advantage_4k: 8.3\n      kl_vs_teal_30_avg_advantage_16k: 9.8\n      task_difficulty_ranking: \"VT > CWE > NIAH (most to least affected by sparsity)\"\n    result_rationale: >\n      RULER 提供 controlled synthetic diagnostics，消除 natural language evaluation\n      的 noise。\n\n      NIAH (retrieval): 最 robust。Dense 4K ≈ 96%（near-perfect）。KL 30% 降至 89%\n      （-7pp），因为 retrieval 主要依赖 attention heads 的 query-key matching，FFN\n      sparsity 仅间接影响 residual stream quality。TEAL 30% (82%) 比 KL 30% (89%)\n      差 7pp，说明 KL masks 更好保护 attention-critical FFN neurons（那些 encode\n      position-dependent information 的 neurons）。16K 下 dense 已降至 92%，KL 30%\n      降至 78%——additional 11pp drop vs 4K 反映 predictor 从 2048 training length 到\n      16K 的 extrapolation degradation（对长程 dependency 的 neuron importance 估计\n      不准确）。\n\n      VT (variable tracking): 最 sensitive。Dense 4K = 88%（本身不完美因为 Llama-8B\n      的 multi-hop reasoning 有限）。KL 30% 4K = 75%（-13pp），degradation 比 NIAH\n      大因为 variable tracking 需要 precise intermediate computation——每层 FFN 的\n      pruned neurons 导致 variable state 的 subtle corruption 在多步 tracking 中\n      累积。KL 50% 4K VT = 52%——接近 random baseline，与 capacity-cliff 一致。\n      16K 进一步降至 KL 30% = 57%，因为更长 context 意味着更多 variable assignment\n      steps，amplifying per-step errors。\n\n      CWE (common words extraction): 中等。需要 global context understanding\n      但不需要 precise multi-hop reasoning。退化模式介于 NIAH 和 VT 之间。\n\n      KL vs TEAL advantage 在 16K 下略微扩大（+9.8pp vs +8.3pp at 4K），说明\n      KL-derived masks 的 information preservation advantage 在长序列下更重要——\n      更多 layers × longer sequences = 更大的 cumulative benefit from better masks。\n      但 median 档下这个 trend 不 dramatic enough to be a standalone finding。\n    tables:\n      - \"Table A.7: RULER Results at 4K and 16K (Appendix)\"\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Figure A.4: RULER accuracy heatmap (rows = method, columns = task_type × context_length), color = accuracy\"\n    analysis: >\n      RULER 在 median 档下是 Appendix material，补充 plan_010 LongBench。主要 unique\n      value: (1) task-type decomposition 明确了 sparsity 对不同 capability 的\n      differential impact（VT > CWE > NIAH），提供 mechanistic insight \"FFN sparsity\n      disproportionately hurts state-tracking and multi-hop reasoning\"。(2) 4K vs\n      16K comparison 量化了 predictor 的 length generalization gap。但如果 plan_010\n      已 cover long-context evaluation，审稿人可能视 RULER 为 redundant。建议：\n      仅在 RULER 的 VT 退化 pattern 产生了 LongBench 未 reveal 的 insight 时放\n      正文，否则 appendix。Median 档下 RULER 不太可能影响 reviewer score。\n```","stop_reason":"end_turn","session_id":"19d5cd1b-dd8d-4261-868b-cdcfcf2d032f","total_cost_usd":1.1946287500000001,"usage":{"input_tokens":4,"cache_creation_input_tokens":46029,"cache_read_input_tokens":108459,"output_tokens":32910,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"